## 1. Introduction
## 1.1 Assembly of MOMs with catalytic relevance
Supramolecular catalysis inherent in natural enzymatic systems has been pursued for decades with the goals to develop ‘‘artificial enzyme catalysts’’ for synthetic chemistry or to understand fundamental mechanisms with regard to enzyme active sites.1
Supramolecular coordination chemistry provides a promising platform for such supramolecular catalytic purposes, for example, to mimic pocket nature or host–guest interactions of enzymes by assembly of various size and shape predefined nanoreactors or molecular flasks.2 Recently, rapid progress in supramolecular catalysis was achieved for porous coordination solids which can offer well-defined coordination nanospace and functional groups/sites appropriate for catalysis.3 The persistent catalytic interest takes the advantage of the coordination assembly which features in good bond directionality, moderate bond energy, self-correcting kinetic reversibility and plentiful metal geometries, thus driving the metalor ligand-directed self-assembly to produce a variety of metal– organic materials (MOMs)4 with varied relevance to catalysis.
