To evaluate the catalytic activity of MIL-101(Cr), Kaskel et al. used the cyanosilylation of aldehydes as a test reaction. $^{23b}\mathrm{MIL}\cdot$ 101(Cr) displayed an excellent activity towards the addition of trimethylsilylcyanide (TMSCN) to benzaldehyde. The framework of MIL-101(Cr) was intact after usage as a catalyst, verified by the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the catalyst after cyanosilylation. To prove the heterogeneity of the reaction, the catalyst was filtered off after $15\mathrm{min}$ of reaction, and stirring of the filtrate was continued at the same reaction temperature. Neither additional benzaldehyde was consumed nor was the product formed after filtration. The recycling test showed that the conversion of the product didn’t reduce remarkably in the second and third runs.
Ahn et al. employed MIL-101(Cr) in selective oxidation of tetralin to 1-tetralone using tert-butylhydroperoxide (TBHP) or acylperoxy radicals generated in situ from trimethylacetaldehyde and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidants.23c They found that both systems can afford 1-tetralone in high yields and selectivity. The authors checked the heterogeneity of the MIL-101(Cr) catalyst. The hot filtration experiment showed that the reaction in the filtrate proceeded with much slower conversion of tetralin than in the presence of the catalyst. ICP-MS also suggested that only a trace amount of chromium leached into the solution $\left(0.12\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ . Furthermore, the MIL-101(Cr) catalyst can be recycled and reused 5 times with the identical catalytic activity and selectivity. Powder XRD patterns of the fresh catalyst and that after the fifth cycle confirmed the structural integrity of MIL-101 during the reaction. The UV-visible diffuse reflectance spectrum of the solid catalyst after reuse was also indistinguishable from that of the fresh dry catalyst, suggesting that no change in the coordination environment of the $\mathrm{Cr}\mathrm{(m)}$ centre took place during the catalytic reaction. Alternatively, Chang and Kim et al. reported that MIL-101(Cr) was catalytically active in the oxidation reactions of aryl sulfides to selectively produce the corresponding sulfoxides in the presence of hydrogen peroxide.24
The potential of MIL-101(Cr) as a heterogeneous catalyst for the oxidation of alkenes, such as cyclohexene (CyH), $\mathfrak{X}$ -pinene and limonene, has been explored by Kholdeeva et al. using TBHP as the oxidant. They found that the main oxidation products were the corresponding $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketones.25 The MIL-101(Cr) catalyst didn’t lose its activity and selectivity after at least five catalytic cycles of CyH oxidation with TBHP. The powder XRD study confirmed the preservation of the MIL-101(Cr) structure after catalytic reactions. The hot catalyst filtration test revealed no further substrate conversion in the filtrate after removal of the catalyst. A trace amount of chromium $\left(<1\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ was determined in the filtrate by ICP-AES.
The application of MIL-101(Cr) in solvent-free coupling of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and epoxides to produce cyclic carbonates was reported by Zalomaeva et al.26 They found that MIL-101(Cr) can promote the reaction under both high-pressure $\left(100\mathrm{atm}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ and lowpressure (8 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\cdot\mathbf{\bar{\Lambda}}$ ) conditions in the temperature range of $25\mathrm{-}120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After optimization, it was found that the presence of tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBABr) as a co-catalyst could largely accelerate the cycloaddition reaction. The heterogeneous nature of catalysis was proved by the hot catalyst filtration test, and no further substrate conversion in the filtrate occurred after catalyst removal at the reaction temperature. However, the catalytic activity tends to decrease after several reuse. Partial regeneration of the catalyst is possible through treatment with hot DMF before use in the next catalytic run.
MIL-100(Fe). Serre et al. reported the synthesis and structure determination of MIL-100(Fe) with the formula of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{F}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}.$ $\mathbf{O}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ (1,3,5-btc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzene tricarboxylic or trimesic acid), which possess two types of mesoporous cages of free apertures of ca. 25 and $29\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ , accessible through microporous windows of ca. 5.5 and $8.6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.^{27}$ The permanent porosity of this porous solid was measured by $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ adsorption experiments performed in liquid nitrogen, revealing an adsorption isotherm characteristic of microporosity. The thermal stability of MIL-100(Fe) has been determined by X-ray thermodiffractometry to be up to $270~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The catalytic activities of MIL-100(Fe) were studied by the same group for the first time. They examined its catalysis in Friedel– Craft benzylation of benzene by benzyl chloride (Fig. 6), and found that MIL-100(Fe) could afford diphenylmethane with high activity and selectivity (nearly $100\%$ ) after a short induction period $\mathrm{(5~min)}$ .
In Garcia’s group, MIL-100(Fe) has been extensively used for catalytic reactions such as benzylic oxidation using tert-butylhydroperoxide (TBHP), ring-opening of styrene oxide with alcohols, Claisen Schmidt condensation reaction, aerobic oxidation of thiophenol to diphenyldisulfide, and the isomerization of $\mathfrak{a}$ -pinene oxide.28 In all kinds of the reactions examined, MIL-100(Fe) could be recycled with no significant loss of catalytic efficiency and crystallinity in subsequent runs.
MIL-53(Al). The well-known and vigorously investigated Al-containing porous framework, MIL-53(Al) $(\mathsf{A l}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{3})$ , was also developed by Fe´rey’s group.29a The crystal structure of MIL-53(Al) is constructed from corner sharing octahedral ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ ions linked by bdc ligands. MIL-53(Al) has the pore dimension of $8.5\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ with good thermal stability and a BET surface area of $1140\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ in its dehydrated and large pore form.
Garcia et al. applied MIL-53(Al) as a heterogeneous catalyst for hydrazine-mediated hydrogenation (Fig. 7).29b Both terminal and internal olefins can be reduced in this catalytic system, giving rise to the corresponding alkanes with high conversion and selectivity $(99-100\%)$ . The reusability of MIL-53(Al) was investigated, showing a gradual decrease in the styrene percentage conversion upon reuse. The powder XRD patterns of the reused MIL-53(Al) sample in four consecutive hydrogenation reactions of styrene to ethylbenzene matched with those of the fresh catalyst, suggesting that the crystallinity of MIL-53(Al) was preserved during the course of the reactions.
MIL-100(Sc). Wright et al. have reported the synthesis of a range of porous scandium carboxylate MOFs with structures analogous to those of MIL-88, MIL-100 and MIL-101 containing different trivalent metals (such as Cr and Fe). The large pore scandium trimesate MIL-100(Sc) $\left(\mathrm{Sc}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right)$ was prepared using the 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ in DMF.30a MIL-100(Sc) contains two types of supercages of free apertures of $c a.\ 25$ and $30\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ , accessible through microporous windows of ca. 5 and 9 Å. The scandium in $\displaystyle\mathrm{Sc}_{3}\mathbf{O}\big(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{C}\big)_{2}.$ $\mathrm{(OH)}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ trimers of MIL-100(Sc) can lose coordinated water to leave five-fold coordinated scandium cations, acting as the strong Lewis acid catalytic sites. Clarke and Wright et al. then applied MIL-100(Sc) in a series of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ bond forming reactions,30b including (i) the intermolecular carbonyl ene reaction of nucleophilic alkenes and electron-poor aldehydes, (ii) a Friedel– Crafts type Michael addition between electron-rich heterocycles and electron-deficient alkenes, and (iii) ketimine and aldimine formation (Fig. 8). In each of these reactions, MIL-100(Sc) was both catalytically active and selective. Filtration experiments and recycle tests confirmed that the catalytic reactions over MIL100(Sc) proceeded in a heterogeneous way.
MIL-47(V). An $\mathrm{{V}(\mathrm{{IV})}}$ -based MOF MIL-47(V) with the formula of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{bdc}})$ has been prepared by Fe´rey et al.31a MIL-47(V) consists of a porous terephthalate framework built from infinite chains of $\mathrm{V}^{4\dagger}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedra and has a three-dimensional (3D) orthorhombic structure, exhibiting large pores $\left(10.5\times11.0\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}\right)$ in the [100] direction. The measured BET and Langmuir surface area are 930(30) and $1320(2)~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
Van Der Voort and Van Speybroeck et al. investigated the catalytic activity of MIL-47(V) in the liquid phase oxidation of cyclohexene (Fig. 9). $^{31b,c}$ The catalytic performance of MIL-47(V) in the water and water-free media using TBHP/water and TBHP/ decane as oxidants, respectively, has been examined. The major oxidized products were cyclohexene oxide, cyclohexane-1,2-diol, tert-butyl-2-cyclohexenyl-1-peroxide and 2-cyclohexen-1-one, and the conversions were around $80\%$ at the end of the reaction. When the oxidant TBHP was dissolved in water, a significant leaching of V-species into the solution was observed. When, however, the oxidant was dissolved in decane, leaching was negligible and the structural integrity of the MIL-47(V) was maintained during successive runs.
2.2 MOFs with commonly used high valent metal ions (Mn, Co, Cu, Zn, and Ag)
Mn-MOFs. Long et al. reported the synthesis and crystal structure of $\mathrm{\small{[Mn(DMF)_{6}]_{3}[(M n_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{10}]_{2}}}$ 1 $[{\bf H}_{3}]\mathrm{btt}=1,3,5\mathrm{-}$ benzenetristetrazol-5-yl), obtained by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathbf{MnCl}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{3}$ btt.32a The desolvated form of the compound, $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ (Mn-btt), was generated by heating the methanol exchanged crystals at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under dynamic vacuum for a minimum of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The X-ray single crystal direction revealed a cubic structure in which chloride-centered square-planar $\mathrm{[{\bf{M}n_{4}C l}]^{7+}}$ units are linked via btt3 ligands to form the anionic, 3D framework, containing 7 and $10\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ pores that afford a BET surface area of $2100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ ions were exposed on the surface of the framework, which might serve as potent Lewis acid sites.
They then applied the Mn-btt MOF in the cyanosilylation and Mukaiyama-aldol reaction (Fig. 10).32b Cyanosilylation of carbonyl-functionalized organic substrates can lead to the formation of cyanohydrins, which are key derivatives in the synthesis of pharmaceuticals. In addition, cyanosilylation can be performed under mild reaction conditions (room temperature). Actually, cyanosilylation of aldehyde has now become a typical reaction to test the Lewis acidity catalysis of CUMs in MOFs. The Mn-btt-catalyzed cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde led to $98\%$ conversion after $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Meanwhile, the Mukaiyamaaldol reaction between benzaldehyde and methyltrimethylsilyldimethylketene acetal led to the aldol product with $63\%$ yield after $^{99\mathrm{h}}$ . To probe whether the cyanosilylation reaction occurred inside the pores or on the surface of the catalyst, substrates 1-naphthaldehyde $(9.7\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\times~}~}8.4\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\mathring{A}^{2}}})$ , 4-phenoxybenzaldehyde $\left(13.3\times7.3\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}\right)$ and 4-phenylbenzaldehyde $(13.1\times6.7\:\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2})$ with increasing molecular dimensions were tested, offering corresponding cyanosilylates in 90, 19 and $18\%$ yield, respectively. The size-selectivity effect suggested that the catalytic reactions might occur in the pores of the MOF catalyst. In addition, infrared spectroscopy of the catalyst impregnated with an aliquot of the reaction solution revealed two C–O stretches at 1698 and $1686~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to free and $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{\mathbf{n}}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ -bound benzaldehyde, respectively, confirming that activation of the substrate occurred at the unsaturated $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{{n}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ sites within the MOF pores.
Wu et al. reported the synthesis and catalytic behaviors of a 3D supramolecular network of $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{2}(p v i a)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]cdot3H_{2}O}}$ (Mn-pvia, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{pvia}=E\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\updownarrow}$ -(2-(pyridin-4-yl)vinyl) isophthalic acid) with cavity dimensions of $7.8\times8.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ .33 The phenylmethanol oxidation was chosen as the model reaction to test the catalytic activity of the desolvated Mn-pvia. The catalytic experiment was performed in acetonitrile at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $\mathrm{NaIO_{4}}$ as the oxidant and in the presence of the desolvated MOF ambient light. After $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the oxidation product was identified as benzaldehyde in $64\%$ yield.
Co-MOFs. Two pyrazolate-based Co-MOFs of $\mathrm{[Co^{II}_{4}O(b d p b)_{3}]}$ (MFU-1, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{b}\ =\ 1,4\cdot$ bis[(3,5-dimethyl)pyrazol-4-yl]-benzene) and $[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bdpb})]$ (MFU-2) were prepared by Volkmer by the solvothermal reactions of $\textstyle\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{b}$ with $\mathbf{CoCl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , respectively.34 The framework of MFU-1 has 3D intersecting channels with an equivalent diameter of $18.1\mathring{\mathbf{A}},^{34a}$ whereas the framework of MFU-2 contains quadratic tunnels with the diagonal length of $18.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ along the $c$ axis. Both MFU-1 and MFU-2 contain redox-active $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers, making them the potentially redox catalysts.34b The variable-temperature XRD patterns indicate that the MFU-1 and MFU-2 frameworks were stable up to 270 and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, withstanding complete removal of solvent molecules.
To investigate the catalytic activities, the authors employed MFU-1 and MFU-2 in the liquid-phase oxidation of cyclohexene using TBHP as the oxidant (Fig. 11). The maximum cyclohexene conversion after $22\mathrm{~h~}$ for MFU-1 and MFU-2 was 27.5 and $16\%$ , respectively, and the main reaction products obtained using both the MOF catalysts were tert-butyl-2-cyclohexenyl-1-peroxide, followed by 2-cyclohexen-1-one and cyclohexene oxide. To confirm the heterogeneous nature of the catalytic reaction, the authors performed hot-filtration experiments. Upon reaching a substrate conversion of approximately $50\%$ , the catalyst particles were removed from the hot solution. The filtrate obtained from the test run containing MFU-1 showed no significant catalytic conversion upon catalyst removal, indicating that contributions of soluble species towards catalytic activity were negligible. In contrast, the filtrate from a reaction mixture containing MFU-2 showed only a slightly diminished activity. The different behavior of two MOFs in hot filtration experiments indicated that MFU-1 acts as a truly heterogeneous catalyst, whereas the catalytic activity of MFU-2 was mostly due to soluble metal complexes leaching from the framework under catalytic conditions. MFU-1 can be almost completely recovered from the reaction vessel and reused in several catalytic runs. Although there was a loss (around $3\%$ ) of catalytic activity after the first run, no further loss in catalytic activity was observed in subsequent runs.
Dietzel and co-workers prepared Co-MOF-74 (also known as CPO-27-Co) with formula $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dhbdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dhbdc}\ =\ 2,5-\right.$ dihydroxyisophthalic acid).35a The crystal structure of MOF-74 was built around a 1D honeycomb motif with pores of $11-12\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ diameter. The CUMs in Lewis acidity could be developed upon removal of the solvent molecules attached to the metal sites by heating in vacuum.
The heterogeneous catalysis of Co-MOF-74 for the cycloaddition of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and styrene epoxide was evaluated by Ahn et al (Fig. 12).35b Co-MOF-74 catalysts can be reused several times with retention of high catalytic activity with $95\%$ conversion. This result is remarkable since $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture has become a worldwide focus because of the greenhouse effect. Cycloaddition of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and epoxides can transform gaseous $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to useful cyclic carbonates. Therefore, this reaction has become very popular in MOF catalysis, especially interesting when the porous MOFs exhibit good $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capabilities, and then the reaction might take place in the cavities.
Stock et al. discovered one microporous MOF Co-btt with the formula of $\mathrm{\small{[Co(DMA)_{6}]_{3}[(C o_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{12}]_{2}\cdot12H_{2}O}}$ by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathbf{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{b}\mathrm{tt}$ in an amide solvent DMA.36 Co-btt adopts a porous 3D framework of 10.9 and $15.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ pores made of square-planar $\mathrm{[Co_{4}C l]^{7+}}$ units interconnected by triangular tritopic $\mathsf{b t t}^{3-}$ bridging ligands. The temperature-dependent XRD analysis showed that the as-synthesized Co-btt was stable up to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Co-btt possesses potential Lewis acid sites as well as redox-active Co sites. Inspired by the rather high thermal stability, the authors tested Co-btt’s catalytic activities in the ring opening of styrene oxide (Fig. 13). Ring opening of epoxides provides a convenient route to 1,2-diols and ${\upbeta}$ -alkoxyalcohols, which can be assisted by mild Lewis acid such as metal cations. Catalytic results showed that Co-btt performed as a better catalyst than the homogeneous catalyst of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . To test the heterogeneity of catalysis, they carried out the hot filtration experiment, finding that after the Co-btt catalyst was filtered off at $30\%$ conversion, the solution in the absence of solid didn’t yield the ring-opening products anymore. Furthermore, Co-btt can be recycled, exhibiting very minor decrease of the conversions upon reuse for the second and third runs.
Cu-MOFs. As early as in 1999, Williams et al. reported the synthesis and crystal structure of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ , also called HKUST-1 (HKUST for Hong Kong University of Science and Technology).37a However, its wide applications in catalysis was not explored until several years later. The structure comprises three types of micropores (9.0, 5.0 and $3.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ ), displaying a cubic network in which $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ -clusters are coordinated by carboxylate groups to give a so-called paddle-wheel unit. Two ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are imposed in the vicinity and the residual axial coordination sites are filled by weakly bound water molecules. These loosely bound water molecules point towards the center of the pore. Removal of the copper-bound water molecules is able to yield CUMs, which can be further applied in catalysis. Concerning its catalytic behaviors, $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ has been used in cyanosilylation of aldehydes, isomerization of terpene derivatives, cyclopropanation of olefins, the Friedla¨nder condensation, Pechmann condensation, and so on (Fig. 14).
For example, Kaskel et al. used the liquid phase cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde as a test reaction to study the selectivity and activity of the porous HKUST-1.37b Before the catalytic reactions, the MOF was dehydrated in high vacuum at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ in order to remove physically and chemically bound water molecules.
Catalytic results showed a cyanosilylation yield of up to $57\%$ . The heterogeneity was proved by filtration experiments at the reaction temperature. Two years later, De Vos et al. found that $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ can be a highly selective Lewis acid catalyst for the isomerization of terpene derivatives, such as the rearrangement of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -pinene oxide to campholenic aldehyde and the cyclization of citronellal to isopulegol.38 The heterogeneity was also proved by filtration experiments, and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ can be used for several runs.
Corma et al. for the first time employed $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ as the catalyst for the cyclopropanation of alkenes with diazoacetates, inducing a carbene transfer reaction from diazo compounds.39 It is known that in homogeneous catalysis, copper complexes are often applied in carbene formation and transformation processes, especially cyclopropanation with olefins. Moreover, copper complexes have been immobilized in polymers or mesoporous silica such as MCM-41 for heterogeneous catalysis. Corma et al. showed that MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ , as a heterogeneous catalyst was active and selective toward the cyclopropanation of alkenes without losing the crystallinity and hence can be reused without further modification. Initially, they studied the reactivity between styrene and ethyl diazoacetate (EDA) in the presence of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ . The catalytic reaction yielded cyclopropane with $98\%$ conversion and $71\%$ diastereoselectivity (trans/cis). Later, they applied the protocol to other diazo compounds such as ethyl 2-phenyldiazoacetate (PhEDA). The reaction with PhEDA showed much better diastereoselectivity, and only a trans-diastereoisomer was formed. The crystalline $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ can be recycled after use. The conversions and yields of the cis- and trans-cyclopropanation products derived from styrene were maintained within three cycles. A comparison of XRD patterns before and after several reaction runs did not reveal any significant differences. The heterogeneity was confirmed by conventional filtration experiment as well as inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometric (ICP-OES) analysis of the filtrate, and little metal leaching was found.
$\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ as the efficient heterogeneous catalyst for the Friedla¨nder reaction under mild reaction conditions was discovered by Cˇejka et al.40a The Friedla¨nder reaction between
2-aminobenzophenone and ketone is an important synthesis tool for the formation of quinolone derivatives, which can be promoted by both Bronsted and Lewis acids. MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ can be recovered after use. XRD evidenced no significant structural changes to the $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ framework after the reaction. They also compared the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ with other heterogeneous catalysts, such as H-BEA and (Al)SBA-15. Under the same experimental conditions, H-BEA and (Al)SBA-15 yielded the quinolone product in only 38 and $36\%$ , respectively, in contrast to $80\%$ conversion over $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ . In a continuing report, they employed the protocol for the synthesis of polysubstituted quinolines with a wide substrate scope.40b
In another report, the same group discovered that $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ can also be applied to the Pechmann condensation of naphthol and ethyl acetoacetate.40c The Pechmann condensation can be used for the synthesis of coumarins, which are important natural substances and have been widely applied in pharmaceutical, agrochemical and fragrance. The condensation was performed under acidic conditions, and almost total transformation of naphthol $93\%$ conversion) to the target product $95\%$ selectivity) was achieved within $20\mathrm{h}$ . For comparison, the Pechmann condensation with naphthol over BEA and USY zeolites led to the coumarin product in 15 and $23\%$ conversion, respectively. The heterogeneity of the catalysis was also proved by filtration experiments.
Asymmetric catalysis has also been achieved by design of chiral Cu-MOFs. Wu et al. developed a chiral serine-derived ligand (S)-3-hydroxy-2-(pyridin-4-ylmethylamino) propanoic acid (papa).41a Self-assembly of papa and $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in a mixed solvent of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and EtOH led to blue crystals of $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{papa})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The crystal structure is a 3D porous framework containing 1D chiral channels $(5.1\times2.9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2})$ ). The cavities are occupied by water molecule guests, which can be easily removed by heating at $110^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{C}$ for several hours. The porous chiral framework of $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{papa})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was maintained after the removal of solvent molecules, and remained stable up to $191^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The authors examined the catalytic activities of $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{papa})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in the Biginelli reaction, and addition of the Grignard reagent to $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketones. The Biginelli reaction is a threecomponent coupling reaction among ethyl acetoacetate, urea and an aryl aldehyde (e.g. benzaldehyde) catalyzed by Lewis acids such as copper salts, yielding dihydropyrimidinones which are important substances with broad applications in the pharmaceutical industry such as calcium channel blockers and antihypertensive agents. In the presence of $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{papa})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}.$ , the Biginelli reaction yielded corresponding dihydropyrimidinone in $90\%$ isolated yield without any enantiomeric excess, whereas 1,2-addition of the Grignard reagent gave excellent conversion (up to $98\%$ ) as well as good ee (up to $99\%$ ). The supernatant from $\mathrm{{\bfCu}}_{2}(\mathrm{papa})_{2}\mathrm{{\bfCl}}_{2}$ was inactive for neither reaction, which confirmed the heterogeneous behavior of the catalyst system.
Later, this group further reported an interesting 3D MOF $\mathrm{\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{3}(p d t c)(p v b a)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}}$ which was obtained from the selfassembly of two kinds of pyridine carboxylates pyridine-2,3,5,6- tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\big(H_{4}p d t c\big)}$ , $(E)$ -4-(2-(pyridin-4-yl)vinyl)benzoic acid (pvba), and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ in a mixed solvent of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and DMF.41b
The crystal structure showed that pdtc and pvba linked up copper atoms to create a 3D framework with very large 1D opening channels of $13.8\mathrm{~\times~}23.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A}}^{2}$ , of which one third of the copper sites are exposed on the interior surface, and three of the five copper coordination sites are occupied by volatile water molecules that can be removed by heating. Since the Henry reaction (also referred to as the nitro-aldol reaction), known as one of the most powerful and atom-economic reactions for C–C bond formation with various functionalized structural motifs, can be efficiently promoted by copper complexes, authors evaluate the catalytic activity of this Cu-MOF in the Henry reaction of aromatic aldehydes with nitromethane under solvent free conditions. The reactions generated the corresponding products in excellent yield (up to $85\%$ ). The heterogeneity of reactions was confirmed by filtration experiments and the ICP analysis of the filtrate. Only $6\times{{10}^{-3}}$ percent of copper ions were released into the solution. The Cu-MOF can be simply recovered by filtration, which was subsequently used in the successive five runs without decreasing the catalytic activity. An XRD pattern of the recovered material showed that the structural integrity of the catalyst was maintained during the catalytic process.
A cage-like copper-containing MOF $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ $2\cdot\mathrm{pymo}=$ 2-hydroxypyrimidinolate) was obtained by Navarro et al. by the reaction of $\mathrm{CuX}_{2}$ salts ( $\mathbf{\widehatX}=\mathbf{Cl}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}\dot{.}$ and 2-pymo HCl in aqueous amine solutions (amine ${\bf\Pi}={\bf N}{\bf H}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ).42a The crystal structure is a distorted 3D sodalite framework, formed by $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -cages accessible through hexagonal windows of about 8 Å. The catalytic activity in 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition reactions (‘‘click’’ reaction) was demonstrated by Corma et al. for MOF $\mathrm{Cu}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ (Fig. 15).42b The reactions of benzyl azide and phenylacetylene in the presence of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ in ethanol at $70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ gave the complete conversion of the benzyl azide after $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , and yielded the solo 1,4-substituted triazole. After the catalytic reaction, the solid MOF was recovered by filtration and thoroughly washed with ethanol. According to the XRD patterns of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ before and after the catalytic reactions, the crystalline nature of the material was preserved. The Cu-MOF can be reused for at least six consecutive runs without loss of activity and selectivity. The heterogeneous nature of the catalytic process was demonstrated by a hot filtration test.
Later, the same group found that $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ was also an active, stable and reusable solid catalyst for three-component coupling of amines, aldehydes and alkynes to form the corresponding propargylamines (Fig. 15).42c After the reaction of propargylamine formation, the solid catalyst was recovered by filtration and washed with dioxane and acetone and dried at room temperature in a desiccator. No significant losses of crystallinity and activity were observed after five runs. No leached Cu was detected in the solution. The hot filtration experiment showed negligible activity in the filtrate.
Recently, a Cu-MOF $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ (bpdc $\v{A D}=4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid; bpy $\v{D A}=\v{A}_{1,4}^{\prime}$ -bipyridyl) has been examined in oxidative $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}$ coupling reactions via $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ activation.43b The synthesis and crystal structure of $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ were reported by James in $2007,^{43c}$ obtained by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , bpdc and bpy in DMSO at $125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ physisorption measurements disclosed that there are three kinds of pores. The pore sizes of two are in the range of $5{-}15\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ and that of the remaining one is between 15 and $50\ {\mathring{\mathbf{A}}}.$ . Not until in 2013 have Phan et al. examined its catalytic activities.43b They found that this Cu-MOF can act as an efficient heterogeneous catalyst for the cross-dehydrogenative coupling reaction of ethers with
2-carbonyl-substituted phenols. The heterogeneity of the catalysis has been confirmed by the filtration experiment. In addition, the Cu-MOF catalyst can be recycled and reused for at least 8 runs.
We have developed a series of catalytically active $\mathrm{{Cu}(_{I})/\mathrm{{Cu}(_{I I})\mathrm{{-}}}}$ MOFs. A robust porous $\mathbf{Cu(\mathrm{_I})-M O F}$ containing $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{I}_{2}$ subunits, $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ (bttp4 $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-triyl triisonicotinate), was developed as depicted in Fig. 17.44a High quality crystals can be readily obtained via the layering of a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ solution of CuI on a $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ solution of bttp4. After thorough structural characterization and gases/vapors adsorption studies of this MOF by means of single-crystal X-ray crystallography, TG and variabletemperature powder XRD, adsorption/desorption isotherms, especially the guest-exchange study in a single-crystal-to-singlecrystal manner, we found that MOF $\mathrm{Cu_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ could be a potentially heterogeneous catalyst owing to its large free 1D channels $(9\times12\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ after considering the van der Waals radii), the ability to facilely exchange bulky guest molecules, and the availability of coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ sites.
Due to its unique feature possessing the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{I}_{2}$ subunits which are located on the pore surface, we subsequently applied this Cu-MOF in the three-component coupling of sulfonyl azide, alkyne and amine for the first time.44b We started the coupling catalysis with tosyl azide $\left(\mathrm{TsN}_{3}\right)$ . Compared to homogeneous CuI-catalyzed reactions, the $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{{I}}_{2}(\mathrm{{bttp}4)\cdot}$ MOF induced three-component coupling reactions displaying obvious size-selectivity.
Afterward, we developed a new family of azide substrates, oxysulfonyl azides, and applied them in the three-component coupling reactions (Fig. 17).44c It was found that oxysulfonyl azides showed better relativities than other azides such as sulfonyl and phosphoryl azides, and we proposed that the reactions should take place in the Cu-MOF pores in such $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}I_{2}}(b t t p4)}$ -catalyzed reactions. To verify this hypothesis, we carried out a series of experiments as well as physical characterization of the MOF catalyst before and after reactions. Accommodation experiments showed that MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ selectively adsorbed PhCCH over CyCCH (cyclohexyl acetylene) and $\boldsymbol{^{t}\mathrm{BuCCH}}$ . The IR spectrum disclosed the presence of $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ bonds of the amidine products, and both EDS and XPS confirmed the presence of S in the recycled catalyst sample. XPS also verified that the $+1$ valence of Cu atoms in $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ remained unchanged after the catalytic reactions, which was ascertained by observation of the same two intense peaks at $932.5\pm0.2$ and $952.5\pm0.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ assignable to $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ components in the $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}I_{2}}(b t t p4)}$ crystallites before and after catalytic reactions. This XPS result also suggested that the recycled $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ catalyst was still active for further reaction. The heterogeneity of the catalysis has been proved by filtration experiments. The recyclability has also been examined, and $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}I_{2}(b t t p4)}$ can be reused for at least three runs.
A structurally flexible porous $\mathbf{Cu(\mathrm{{u})\mathrm{{-}\mathbf{\mathrm{{MOF}}}}}}$ , $[\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bped})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}.$ $\mathrm{(SiF_{6})}]\supset4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(\mathbf{Cu-MOF-SiF}_{6}\right)$ , was obtained from meso-1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)-1,2-ethanediol and ${\mathrm{CuSiF}}_{6}$ (Fig. 18). $^{44d}\mathrm{Cu\mathrm{-MOF\mathrm{-}S i F_{6}}}$ is a 3D polycatenated porous framework consisting of 2D (4,4) topological grid networks which display 2-fold inclined interpenetration in the crystal lattice. $\sin^{2-}$ anions are hosted in the 1D channels of $\mathbf{Cu-MOF-SiF_{6}}$ . The anion exchange of $\sin(\mathtt{s i F}_{6}^{2-}$ anions with ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ anions led to an isomorphous MOF, $\mathbf{Cu-MOF-NO_{3}}$ . The 3D polycatenated frameworks show dynamic structural behaviour due to the presence of diol groups and the different anions. For $\mathbf{Cu{\mathrm{-}}M O F{\mathrm{-}}S i F_{6}}$ with $\sin\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}_{6}}^{2-}$ anions, a kinetic trap may be generated for MeOH, but bigger EtOH guest molecules are blocked. In comparison, $\mathbf{Cu-MOF-NO_{3}}$ with smaller ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ anions has enlarged voids and exhibit stepwise MeOH and EtOH sorption. The MOFs efficiently oxidize benzylic compounds to the corresponding carbonyl functionality under mild and convenient reaction conditions due to the presence of favourable host– guest interactions and potential coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ centers. Their catalytic properties are anion-responsive and Cu-MOF- ${\bf\cdot N O}_{3}$ showed better catalytic activity than $\mathbf{Cu{\mathrm{-}}M O F{\mathrm{-}}S i F_{6}}$ . The catalysts can be reused with the framework intact for at least three times without loss of any activity.
Phosphines are excellent ligands for soft transition metal and they are ubiquitous in homogeneous catalysis. Using a pyridyl phosphine ligand, 4-(3,5-bis(diphenylphosphino)phenyl)- pyridine, we obtained a series of 3D $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ -MOFs (Fig. 19).44e
These phosphine-containing MOFs are isostructural based on rigid $\mathbf{L}_{2}\mathbf{M}_{2}$ dimeric secondary building blocks with $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ (CuPhos-Br), $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ (CuPhos-Cl) or $\mathrm{PF_{6}}^{-}$ (CuPhos-PF6) as counteranions. The complexes have a 3-connected $4{\cdot}12^{2}$ chiral net topology, which can be further simplified to $6^{4}{\cdot}8^{2}$ -qtz. The MOFs contain 1D homochiral channels to host different counteranions. The MOFs show anion-tunable flexible porosity. For example, CuPhos-Br reveals gradual MeOH uptake, while CuPhos- $\cdot\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ exhibits stepwise MeOH sorption. Due to the presence of potential coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ centers, the MOFs show heterogeneous Lewis acid catalytic activity. The ketalization reactions between ethylene glycol and 2-butanone/ cyclohexanone proceeded up to $93\%$ yield with $0.2\mathrm{mol}\%$ catalyst (CuPhos-Br or CuPhos- $\cdot\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{}}$ ) loading. Profound size selectivity was observed for the MOFs. No reaction happens between ethylene glycol and bulky benzophenone. The MOF catalysts can be reused with the framework left intact for three runs without loss of activity.
Zn-MOFs. Zn-containing MOFs, which represent one of the most popularly studied MOF materials, have also been utilized as potential candidates in supramolecular heterogeneous catalysis. However, compared to the essential roles of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ion in natural enzymes and wide exploration of its catalytic activities in organic synthesis methodology, Zn-MOF-catalyzed reactions may still wait for further development. Fedin and Kim et al. reported a homochiral MOF $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{Z n}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-lac})(\mathsf{d m f})]\cdot(\mathsf{d m f})$ , which was prepared from the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , L-lactic acid $\left(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{lac}\right)$ , and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}\right)$ in DMF at $110^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days.45 Single-crystal X-ray analysis revealed that the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions and lactate ligands form 1D chiral chains running along the $a$ axis. The coordinated DMF ligands and the guest DMF molecules can be readily removed at elevated temperatures without causing structural collapse. The authors found that desolvated samples were capable of mediating highly size- and chemoselective catalytic oxidation of thioethers to sulfoxides by urea hydroperoxide (UHP) or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Thioethers with smaller substituents exhibited reasonable conversion (up to $64\%$ ) and high selectivity (up to $92\%$ ) after $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ of catalytic reaction, whereas thioethers with bulkier substituents exhibited very poor conversion. The MOF catalyst can perform at least 30 catalytic cycles without loss of oxidation selectivity.
Another chiral Zn-MOF applicable in asymmetric catalysis was reported by Glorius and Kaskel et al.46 They prepared chiral tricarboxylic acids with a btb (btb $^{=1,3,5}$ -benzenetrisbenzoate) backbone substituted by chiral oxazolidinones ( $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{3}$ chirbtb-n), namely 1,3,5-tri{4-[2-(4-isopropyl-2-oxooxazolidin-3-yl)]benzoate}- benzene $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}{\mathrm{chirbtb}}-1\right)$ and $^{1,3,5-\mathrm{tri}\{4}}$ -[2-(4-benzyl-2-oxooxazolidin-3-yl)]benzoate}benzene $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{chirbtb-}2)$ ). Solvothermal reactions of zinc nitrate and $\mathbf{H_{3}c h i r b t b{-1}}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{3}$ chirbtb-2 in diethylformamide (DEF) at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led to crystallization of Zn-MOFs $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb}{\cdot}1)_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-2})_{2}$ , respectively. MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-1})_{2}$ has large-sized pores with approximately $33.7\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ in diameter, whereas MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb}{-}2)_{2}$ possesses rectangular channels of $18\ \times\ 18\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $c$ -axis. Compared to other Lewis acid catalysis such as cyanosilylation, Mukaiyama aldol reaction required stronger Lewis acid catalysts. In previously mentioned Mukaiyama aldol reactions catalyzed by Mn-btt, the metal nodes were achiral, so the obtained aldol products didn’t exhibit any enantiomeric excess. To challenge the MOFs in asymmetric Mukaiyama aldol reaction, both $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-1})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-2})_{2}$ MOFs were tested (Fig. 20). $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-1})_{2}$ was found to be efficient in reaction using benzaldehyde. The enantioselectivity with $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-1})_{2}$ as an asymmetric catalyst during reaction depended on the solvent. If $n$ -heptane was used, an enantiomeric excess of $9\%$ was detected, but no influence on the enantioselectivity of the product could be observed when dichloromethane was used. $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{chirbtb-2})_{2}$ catalyzed the reaction of benzaldehyde and the silyl enol ether with a decreased reaction rate, and a small but reproducible enantiomeric excess of $6\%$ ( $\overset{\cdot}{n}$ -heptane) and $8\%$ (dichloromethane), respectively, was measured.
A series of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) have been developed and well characterized by Yaghi and others. Among them, ZIF-8 attracted much attention because of its large pores of $11.6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ that are accessible through small apertures of $3.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{47a}$ Furthermore, ZIF-8 is an appealing material to employ as a catalyst for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion to cyclic carbonates because of the presence of Lewis acid sites in its framework.
Carreon et al. reported the catalytic performance of ZIF-8 and amino-functionalized ZIF-8 in the synthesis of chloropropene carbonate from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and epichlorohydrin (Fig. 21).47b ZIF-8 was prepared at room temperature from zinc nitrate hexahydrate and 2-methylimidazole, whereas the functionalized ZIF-8 was prepared by post-modification of dried ZIF-8 with ethylene diamine. The ZIF-8 catalysts, especially those surface-modified with amines, display high epoxide conversions and moderate to high selectivities to the cyclic carbonate at temperatures even as low as ${70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}}$ The presence of both the $\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}$ acid sites and the N basic moieties (in adjacent locations) from the imidazole linker in ZIF-8 probably facilitated the adsorption of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ on the solid surface and its further conversion to the carbonate. However, the catalytic activity of the recycled catalysts (both nonfunctionalized and amine-functionalized) decreased in the second and third run. As shown in the powder XRD patterns, although ZIF-8 structure was preserved after the first cycle, the recycled catalysts lost their structural features after the second cycle. On the other hand, the BET surface area decreased from 1173 to $772~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the nonfunctionalized ZIF-8 and from 1096 to $915~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the functionalized ZIF-8. This may suggest pore blockage of the recycled ZIF-8 by carbonaceous material formed during the reaction, and may explain in part the lower activity of the recycled catalysts.
Ag-MOFs. The application of Ag-MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis is still in its infancy. Duan’s group reported a homochiral MOF Ag-tpha (tpha $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tris(4-(1-(2-pyridin-2-ylhydrazono)- ethyl)-phenyl) amine) with the formula of $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{3}(\mathrm{tpha})_{2}\right]\cdot3\mathrm{BF}_{4},$ which was prepared by the self-assembly of tpha and $\mathrm{\bfAgBF_{4}}$ in methanol.48 The structure has a 3D framework containing pores of $7.5\times8.0\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ cross section. The distorted tetrahedral Ag centers were located within the pores, which were able to react with suitable substrates. Recent research findings show that in the presence of chiral ligands, Ag(I) complexes could promote the asymmetric cycloaddition reactions of azomethine ylides and electron-deficient olefins, leading to the formation of chiral pyrrolidines in good yields and high enantioselectivities. To explore the potential of Ag-MOFs in asymmetric catalysis, homochiral Ag-tpha1 and Ag-tpha2 were prepared using the same procedure as Ag-tpha by using $(+).$ -cinchonine and $(-)\cdot$ cinchonidine as the chiral adducts, respectively. MOFs Ag-tpha1 and Ag-tpha2 were then employed in the 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition of methyl-2(benzylidene amino)- acetate (MBA) and $N_{\sun}$ -methylmaleimide (NMM). Catalytic results showed that the cycloadduct was obtained with $90\%$ and $-82\%$ ee in the presence of Ag-tpha1 and Ag-tpha2, respectively (Fig. 22). Recycling experiments also showed that Ag-tpha1 can be used at least for three runs and there is no loss of the yields and enantioselectivity.
2.3 MOFs with other uncommonly used metal ions (Mg, Zr, Pd, Ce, and Bi)
Mg-MOFs. Comparing with the commonly used metal ions introduced in Section 2.2, the MOFs constructed from other metals are relatively less. Nevertheless, various catalytic reactions have been investigated by utilizing the MOFs containing specific metal ions, which, in some cases, may exhibit a unique role in heterogeneous catalysis. The uniqueness of Mg-based MOFs may lie in the lightness of the $\mathbf{Mg}$ atom, which could offer relatively high pore density. Koner et al. have reported a new approach of pH-controlled synthesis of MOFs involving $\mathbf{Mg}$ and pyrazole-3,5-dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}{\mathsf{p d c1}}\right)$ , which allows a progressive increase of dimensionality from zero- to 3D frameworks.49a When the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the reaction mixture was adjusted to the basic range $\mathrm{(pH~\approx~8)}$ , a 3D open framework of $\mathrm{{\bfMg}_{3}p d c1(O H)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ (Mg-pdc1) crystallized. The desolvated $\mathbf{Mg-MOF}$ was subjected to aldol condensation reactions of various aromatic aldehydes with acetone (Fig. 23). The results showed that nitro-substituted benzaldehydes were converted into their respective $\upbeta$ -aldol products as the sole products within $^\mathrm{~6~h~}$ . With benzaldehyde and $p$ -methoxybenzaldehyde, however, trans-benzylideneacetone was obtained without formation of the $\upbeta$ -aldol product. No conversion was observed in the absence of the MOF catalyst. To verify that the catalysis was indeed heterogeneous, they performed the hot filtration test. To check the stability of the catalyst, they characterized the recovered material using powder XRD and IR spectral analysis. Comparison of the IR spectra and XRD patterns of the pristine compound and the recovered catalyst convincingly demonstrated that the structural integrity was retained after the aldol condensation reaction. For the recycling study, an aldol condensation reaction was carried out with $p$ -methoxybenzaldehyde and $p$ -nitrobenzaldehyde for three successive runs. The catalytic efficiency of the recovered catalyst remained almost the same in each run.
Later, they demonstrated the synthesis and catalytic behaviors of another 3D $\mathbf{Mg}$ -MOF of $\mathbf{Mg(pdc2)(H_{2}O)}$ (Mg-pdc2, $\mathbf{H}_{2}{\mathsf{p d c}}2=$ pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylic acid) with a rectangle type channel $\left(12.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times\mathrm{~}\left(3.6\mathrm{~\rightarrow~}5.7\right).\right.$ Å).49b They employed this new Mg-MOF in the aldol condensation reactions of aromatic aldehydes and aliphatic ketones such as acetone, cyclohexanone and tert-butylcyclohexanone (Fig. 23). Catalytic results showed that the aldehyde substrates are converted to their respective ${\upbeta}$ -aldols as the sole product. In the presence of Mg-pdc2, they noticed that $\upbeta$ -aldol products did not undergo further transformation to form unsaturated carbonyl compounds, which were different from Mg-pdc1-catalyzed reactions. Mg-pdc2 can also be recycled and reused several times without significant loss of activity.
Zr-MOFs. Synthesis of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOFs seemed to remain a formidable challenge in the 1990s at the beginning of MOFs crystal engineering. However, the discovery that the Zr-based MOFs can be prepared and handled conveniently in atmospheric moisture promoted the development and utilization of Zr-MOFs, which were found to possess enhanced stability, compared to $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs, probably due to the strong $\mathbf{\deltaZr{-}O}$ bonds within the secondary building unit (SBU) and the high degree of interlinking of these SBUs. The strikingly high thermal stability of the porous framework (up to $540~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and excellent chemical stability make $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOFs superior candidates as heterogeneous catalysts to most of the other metal-containing MOFs suffering from low thermal and chemical stability. In 2008, the Lillerud group developed a new Zr-MOF UiO-66 (UiO for University of Olso) with the formula of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b d c)_{12}}$ , which was readily prepared under solvothermal conditions at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ in DMF starting from $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ and terephthalic acid.50a The framework is comprised of tetrahedral and octahedral cages, in a $_{2:1}$ ratio, of free dimensions close to 8 and $11\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ , respectively. Access to the cages is provided by triangular windows with a free diameter of $6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.$ . UiO-66 consists of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ clusters in which the triangular faces of the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ -octahedron are alternatively capped by $\upmu_{3}–\mathbf{O}$ and $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups (Fig. 24). Dehydroxylation of the cluster starts at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and can be completed at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and two of the four $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups leave together with the hydrogen from the remaining two $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups, resulting in a $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ inner cluster. During the process, the overall framework structure is virtually unchanged. This ability to maintain the framework upon dehydroxylation and change in coordination around $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ assisted the application of UiO-66 in catalysis.
De Vos et al. studied the catalytic activity of UiO-66 and the amino-modified $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 in cross aldol condensation reaction.50b $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 can be prepared by the same procedure as that of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 with simple replacement of terephthalic acid by 2-aminoterephthalic acid. The UiO-66 materials were evaluated as catalysts in the synthesis of jasminaldehyde (JA, a-n-amylcinnamaldehyde) by the reaction between heptanal (HA) and benzaldehyde (BA). Before catalytic reactions, both UiO-66 and $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 were pretreated by heating at high temperatures. The results showed that $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 performed better with $10\%$ higher activity and selectivity than UiO-66. Hot filtration tests proved that the catalytic activity was associated with the solid exclusively other than the liquid phase.
Afterward, Van Speybroeck and De Vos employed a series of UiO-66 MOFs with different benzene-1,4-dicarboxylates (bdc-X; $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H} $ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ , F, Cl, Br, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ ) in the cyclization of citronellal, and studied the effects of substituents (Fig. 24).50c Before reaction, each catalyst was activated at $493\ \mathrm{K}.$ . Catalytic results showed that the rate was dramatically enhanced by electron-withdrawing groups on the linker (F, Cl, Br, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}\mathrm{,\Omega}$ , and $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was by far the most active material, reaching full conversion after only $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . They attempted to correlate the activity to electronic effects of the linkers using Hammett’s $\sigma_{m e t a}$ values. The strong positive correlation of the $\log k$ values with $\sigma_{m e t a}$ results in a linear free-energy relationship (LFER, $\log(k_{\mathrm{X}}/k_{\mathrm{H}})=\rho\sigma_{m e t a})$ with $\rho=2.35$ . This is the first LFER ever observed for MOF catalysts. Recently, De Vos developed a synthesis method of UiO-66 by adding trifluoroacetic acid (TFA), in which a part of bdc was substituted by trifluoroaceta.50d They applied the new $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOF in the ‘‘ene’’-type cyclization of citronellal to isopulegol. Results showed that the addition of small amounts of TFA could significantly increase the activity.
Pd-MOFs. Palladium is an intensively investigated metal atom in a broad range of metal catalyzed reactions; however, the Pd-based MOFs are rarely observed in literature. The difficulty in crystal growth of Pd-MOFs may relate to the specific kinetics of Pd-coordinative bonds; therefore, the potential applications of wide Pd-based catalytic activities in Pd-MOFs are seeking for full growth of Pd-MOFs crystal engineering. Navarro and Masciocchi et al. synthesized a novel, 3D microporous sodalitic network, $\mathbf{Pd}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ , which was thermally stable up to $330~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air.51a As shown in its crystal structure, $\mathrm{Pd}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ possessed two different hexagonal windows with free openings of 4.8 and $8.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and with a fraction of $42\%$ of the crystal volume available to adsorbed guests.
The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ was tested by Corma et al. in the Suzuki $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ coupling, alcohol oxidation, and olefin hydrogenation (Fig. 25).51b In the case of Suzuki $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ cross coupling reaction, the reaction between phenylboronic acid and 4-bromoanisole was chosen as the model reaction. The high values of conversion $(85\%)$ and selectivity toward the cross-coupling product $(99\%)$ are obtained after $^\mathrm{~5~h~}$ using $\mathbf{Pd}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It can be reused without significant diminution of catalyst activity and selectivity. The X-ray diffractograms of used Pd-MOF were almost identical to those of fresh samples. In light of alkene hydrogenation, they chose the reactants to explore the potential shape-selective properties of the material, and compared the hydrogenation rates of 1-octene and cyclododecene. Results showed that 1-octene had very high reactivity whereas cyclododecene didn’t work at all, indicating that the active centers located at the internal surface were accessible through the pore openings for 1-octene, whereas cyclododecene could not diffuse inside the pores.
The liquid-phase hydrogenation of 1-octene and cyclododecene using $\mathbf{Pd}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ as a catalyst has also been examined by Klemm et al.51c,d They found that in less than $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , the sizeselectivity was indeed observed; however, when the reaction mixture was exposed to hydrogen for more than $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , the hydrogenation of cyclododecene also took place. Through careful studies they concluded that the phenomenon was caused by partial reduction of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ centers to $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ nanoparticles. The latter could leach out from the frameworks and then promoted the hydrogenation of larger olefins such as cyclododecene.
Ce-MOFs. The lanthanide-containing MOFs (Ln-MOFs) are actually by no means rare compared with Mg-, $Z\mathbf{r}\mathbf{-}$ or Pd-MOFs. However, endeavors in heterogeneous catalysis by using Ln-MOFs are limited, probably because of the intrinsic attribute of the labile Ln-coordinative bonds. Duan et al. synthesized two homochiral
Ln-MOFs Ce-mdip1 and Ce-mdip2 through the solvothermal reactions of methylenediisophthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}m d i p)}$ and $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the presence of L-bcip and D-bcip (bcip $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ N-tert-butoxy-carbonyl-2-(imidazole)-1-pyrrolidine), respectively.52 In compound Ce-mdip1 the mdip ligands linked the metal ions through the carboxyl groups forming a noninterpenetrating 3D network containing chiral channels with a $10.5\ \times\ 6.0\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ cross section along the $a$ -axis. The Ce ions having removable coordinated water molecules were well-positioned within the channels. The crystal structure of Ce-mdip2 is similar to that of Ce-mdip1, but it exhibits Cotton effects exactly opposite to those of Ce-mdip1. The authors examined the catalytic activities of Ce-mdip1 and Ce-mdip2 in the asymmetric cyanosilylation reactions of aromatic aldehydes and cycanotrimethlysilane (Fig. 26). Results showed that the reactions in the presence of each catalyst can achieve high conversion $(>95\%)$ in $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and excellent ee (up to $98\%$ ee), although the reactions led to the formation of opposite major enantiomers. The authors also examined heterogeneity of the reactions. After removal of the catalyst in $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , the filtrate afforded only $10\%$ addition conversion upon stirring for another $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The catalysts can be recycled and reused three times with a slight decrease in reactivity and enantioselectivity. The powder XRD patterns of recycled catalysts matched with those of as-synthesized catalysts.
Bi-MOFs. Bismuth is nontoxic, noncarcinogenic and relatively inexpensive metal, and thus Bi compounds are used as green catalysts. Nevertheless, the Bi-based MOFs are rather uncommon either in synthesis or in application study. Kolb and Stock et al. reported a rare Bi-MOF Bi(btb), which was synthesized by the reaction of $\mathbf{Bi}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}5\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ with $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{t}\mathbf{b}$ in methanol at $120~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ 53 Bi(btb), denoted as CAU-7 ( $\mathbf{\hat{C}A U=}$ Christian-Albrechts-Universita¨t). The crystal structure of CAU-7 has 1D channels with the size of approximately $10{\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}}.$ The temperature-dependent XRD measurements confirmed the thermal stability of the crystalline framework up to $380~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The catalytic activity of CAU-7 was demonstrated in the solvent-free hydroxymethylation of hemicellulose-derived 2-methylfuran to 5-methylfurfuryl alcohol (Fig. 27). Before the catalytic tests, the authors examined the IR spectra of CAU-7 after exposure to acetonitrile, and the IR spectra showed that $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ can be chemically adsorbed onto CAU-7, which suggests that CAU-7 possesses mild Lewis acid sites. Due to the presence of such catalytically active sites, CAU-7 performed well in the hydroxymethylation reaction of 2-methylfuran, leading to $75\%$ yield with $90\%$ conversion. The activity of CAU-7 can be comparable to other MOFs with Lewis or Brønsted acidity, such as UiO-66 and MIL-53(Ga).
In summary, the organic reactions, which are used to test the catalytic abilities of MILs, are focused on oxidation reactions, whose substrates include unsaturated/saturated hydrocarbons, thiols and sulfides. Cyclohexene oxidation is especially interesting as it can yield several products such as epoxide, diol, peroxide and enone, depending on the catalyst and the reaction conditions. Both of $\mathbf{MIL-}47(\mathbf{V})^{31b,c}$ and $\mathbf{MIL-}101(\mathbf{Cr})^{25}$ can efficiently promote cyclohexene oxidation in the presence of TBHP, yielding enone with high selectivity. Oxidation of secondary $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ bonds, especially to benzylic compounds, could lead to mixtures of alcohols and ketones. When using MIL-100(Fe)28a or $\mathbf{MIL-101(Cr)^{24}}$ as the catalyst, the major products were ketones with the selectivity up to $99\%$ . In the case of sulfide oxidation, MILs again display high selectivity. Only sulfoxide of sulfide was catalyzed by MIL-101(Cr), whereas sulfonation of sulfide couldn’t take place in the presence of MIL-101(Cr). As shown in these examples, MILs can be the first choices for the highly selectively oxidative catalysts.
Pleasantly, MOFs, which are constructed with low covalent metal cations such as $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+/2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ , showed diversity in catalysis. Cyanosilylation of aldehydes, Mukaiyamaaldol reaction and ring-opening epoxides are among the most studied reactions to test the Lewis acidity of CUMs in MOFs. $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ is redox active, and thus $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ -MOFs are usually used for the oxidation catalysts. It is noted that Cu and Ag complexes are good homogeneous catalysts for a lot of valuable organic transformations. As a result, there are more reactions that can be used to examine the catalytic activities of Cu- and Ag-MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts.
In general, although the MOFs in Section 2.3 are not common, they could show potentially powerful heterogeneous catalytic activities, especially MOFs that are constructed with $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions, as viewed from the standpoints of methodology. As Pd complexes have been widely applied for $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ cross-coupling reactions as well as $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ bond activation reactions, their versatile potential in catalytic application may wait for reasonable design and synthesis of more stable Pd-MOFs.
## 3. MOFs with metalloligands
An attractive feature of MOFs as supramolecular heterogeneous catalysts is the potential to incorporate metal complexes that have been proven as effective homogeneous catalysts, particularly metallosalens and metalloporphyrins, into the micropores.54–61 Compared to other immobilized catalysts, the metalloligand-based MOFs can functionalize as heterogeneous asymmetric catalysts with higher catalyst loading and more accessible catalytic centers. Table 2 lists the reported MOFs with metalloligands, the window or channel size, and catalytic reactions. The information about the organic ligands in metalloligand structures is shown in Scheme 1.
Metalloporphyrins should be ideal catalytic struts in MOFs, given their rigidity and well-studied catalytic behavior.54a One of their important features lies in the characteristic diversity which can be obtained through the addition of a variety of central metal entities, or via the introduction of functional peripheral substituents. A series of $\mathrm{Ru-/Mn{\cdot}/C o}$ -porphyrin complexes have displayed excellent activities in asymmetric epoxidation, cyclopropanation and aziridination of olefins, as well as $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ bond activation of carbonhydrogens. Hupp,55 Ma,56 ${\mathrm{\DeltaWu}}^{57}$ and Zhou58 groups prepared $\mathbf{Mn{\mathrm{-}}}/\mathbf{Co{\mathrm{-}}}/\mathbf{P}\mathbf{d}{\mathrm{-}}/\mathbf{Fe}$ -porphyrin-based MOFs, and illustrated their catalytic performances in oxidation reactions.
On the other hand, chiral salen ligands, such as $^{(R,R)-1,2-}$ cyclohexanediamino- $\cdot N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-tert-butyl-salicylidene), have been established as one of the few privileged ligand systems for asymmetric catalysis. Some examples of metallosalen-based catalysis include the asymmetric ring-opening reactions of epoxides and aziridines catalyzed by Co(salen) and Cr(salen) complexes, the enantioselective addition of alkynes to ketones catalyzed by Zn(salen) complexes, and the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides catalyzed by both Ti(salen) and V(salen) complexes.54b Motivated by excellent asymmetric catalytic activities exhibited by homogeneous metallosalen complexes, MOFs containing chiral metallosalen building blocks have attracted a great deal of recent interest. So far, only a few chiral metallosalen-based MOFs have been examined in asymmetric catalytic reactions. For example, Hupp, $^{59}\mathrm{Lin}^{60}$ and $\mathrm{\cui}^{61}$ groups constructed chiral MOFs based on $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn-/Ru-/Co}$ -salenderived bipyridine or bicarboxylate ligands and demonstrated their asymmetric catalytic activity in alkene epoxidation, cyclopropanation and hydrolytic kinetic resolution of epoxides.
## 3.1 Metalloporphyrin-based MOFs
Hupp’s group developed a porphyrin-based ligand with pyridine terminal groups, 5,15-dipyridyl-10,20-bis(pentafluorophenyl)- porphyrin (por-1).55a By combining 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)- benzene $\mathrm{(H_{4}t c p b)}$ and por-1 with a Zn salt, a noninterpenetrated, pillared-paddlewheel MOF was built with the formula $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{tcpb})(\mathrm{Zn}\cdot\mathrm{por}\cdot1)$ (ZnPO-MOF). The structure has large channels in three directions that are occupied by a substantial amount of disordered solvent, including $15\times9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ channels along the $a$ axis, $11\times9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ channels along the $b$ axis, and $8\times9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ channels along the $c$ axis. In the acyl-transfer reaction between $N\cdot$ -acetylimidazole and 3-pyridylcarbinol, a 2420-fold rate enhancement was observed in the presence of ZnPO-MOF relative to the uncatalyzed reaction (Fig. 28).
This group subsequently described a facile synthesis method of MOFs that can incorporate a variety of metalloporphyrins, specifically ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{3+}$ complexes.55b Reaction of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , por-1 and 5,10,15,20-tetra(carboxyphenyl)metalporphyrin (por-2) in the presence of $0.03\mathrm{~M~HNO}_{3}$ resulted in the metalloporphyrin-based MOF with formula of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{por}{-}1)(\mathrm{por}{-}2)$ . Isostructural materials can be prepared when the free-base porphyrin por-1 was replaced with the Mn-por-1 complex, and/or the Al-por-2, Pd-por-2, or Fe-por-2 complex was used instead of free ligand por-2. Analysis of single-crystal data revealed noncatenated frameworks of these isostructural materials. The center-to-center distance between cofacial struts derived from por-1 was $16.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , while the distance between cofacial por- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot2{\bf\vec{s}}$ is $22\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ . $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{(Mn{\cdot}p o r{-}1)}$ (Zn-por-2) was selected to demonstrate the catalytic activity. Using a soluble analogue of iodosylbenzene, the MOF was found to be an effective catalyst for both the epoxidation of styrene and hydroxylation of cyclohexane.
$\mathrm{Zn_{2}(Z n{\cdot}p o r{-}1)(Z n{\cdot}p o r{-}2)}$ can be prepared by a similar procedure.55c They found that $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(M{\cdot}p o r{-}1)(Z n{\cdot}p o r{-}2)}$ $\left(\mathbf{M}\right)=\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ , none, $\mathrm{Sn^{4+},A l^{3+},C o^{3+})}$ could be prepared from solvent-assisted linker exchange reactions between $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(Z n{\cdot}p o r{-}1)(Z n{\cdot}p o r{-}2)}$ and M-por-1. Among them, $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(A l\mathrm{-por\mathrm{-}1})(Z n\mathrm{-}p o r\mathrm{-}2)}$ displayed good performances in the ring-opening reaction of epoxides with $\mathbf{TMSN}_{3}$ .
A novel octatopic porphyrin ligand, tetrakis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-porphine (por-3), was designed by Ma’s group.56a Solvothermal reaction of the por-3 and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in dimethylacetamide (DMA) at $115~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ yielded a porphyrin-based MOF $\{[\mathrm{CO_{3}(O H)(H_{2}O)}]_{4}\mathrm{Co(por-3)}_{3}\}\cdot(\mathrm{H}_{2}O)_{20}\cdot(\mathrm{CH}_{3}O H)_{22}\cdot(\mathrm{DMA})_{25}$ (denoted as MMPF-2, $\mathbf{MMPF}=$ metal–metalloporphyrin framework). Crystal structure possesses two rectangular channels with a size of $10\ \times\ 10\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ direction. They concurrently designed another porphyrin ligand 5,15-bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)- 10,20-bis(2,6-dibromophenyl)porphyrin (por-4).56b A new MOF with the formula $\{\mathrm{[Co_{2}(\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{k}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{4}C o(\mathrm{por}\mathrm{-}4)}\}$ solvents (denoted as MMPF-3) was prepared solvothermally from por-4 and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ . MMPF-3 crystallized in the same space group as fcu-MOF-1, which possessed three types of polyhedral cage: a cubohemioctahedron with a window size of $c a.5.9\mathring\mathbf{A}$ and inner dimensions of about $7.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ (atom-to-atom distance), a truncated tetrahedron and a truncated octahedron with a window size of about $9.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and a cavity with an internal volume of about $4000\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ . The catalytic activities of MMPF-2 and MMPF-3 were examined in the epoxidation of trans-stilbene using TBHP as the oxidant in acetonitrile at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Catalytic results disclosed that MMPF-2 converted $67.2\%$ of the substrate after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , whereas MMPF-3 demonstrated a highly efficient catalytic activity in terms of both yield $95.7\%$ over $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ) and selectivity $(87.1\%$ epoxide product) (Fig. 29).
By employing two porphyrin ligands, tetra(carboxyphenyl)- metalporphyrin (por-2) and tetrapyridylporphyrin (por-5), Wu’s group synthesized new porphyrin-based MOFs.57 The porous $\mathrm{{\bfCd}}_{1.25}(\mathrm{Pd}{\cdot}\mathrm{por}{-2})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}){\bf\Delta M O F}^{57\alpha}$ contains two kinds of channels with dimensions of $4.6\times12.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $8.3\ \times\ 9.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $a$ axis, showing significant catalytic activity for styrene oxidation using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as the oxidant (Fig. 30). On the other hand, the reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ with $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}(\mathbf{p}\mathbf{or}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{5})$ in DMF yielded the zwitterionic complex $\mathrm{\{[Mn^{III}(D M F)_{2}T P y P](P W_{12}O_{40})\}^{2-}}_{\mathrm{~:}}$ which subsequently reacted with $\mathbf{Cd}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in a mixed solvent of DMF and acetic acid at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , leading to the formation of $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}(\mathrm{por}-5)](\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}).^{57b}$ The framework of this compound was porous upon the removal of the solvent, displaying large cavities with dimensions of $10\times10\times12\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ and 1D channel openings with dimensions of $5\times12\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the [110] direction. MOF $\mathrm{\big[Cd\big(DMF)_{2}M n\big(D M F)_{2}\big(p o r-5\big)\big]\big(P W_{12}O_{40}\big)}$ displayed excellent capability for highly selective oxidation of alkylbenzenes using TBHP as the oxidant, giving rise to the corresponding ketones in excellent yields with $100\%$ selectivity.
Two more studies of Wu’s group were published in 2013 about the catalytic MOFs constructed with metalloporphyrin. $57c,d$ Porous metalloporphyric MOFs $\mathbf{M}_{2}^{\prime}(\mathbf{HOOC})(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-por}\mathbf{-}2)$ , $\mathbf{\left(M=Mn^{3+}\right.}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ; $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ or $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ), were prepared from solvothermal reactions of M-por-2 and $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})_{2}.\mathbf{\bar{5}}^{7c}\mathbf{M}_{2}^{\prime}\big(\mathbf{HOOC}\big)(\mathbf{Mn}\mathbf{\bar{-}}\mathbf{por}\mathbf{-}2)$ can perform as a recyclable heterogeneous catalyst in epoxidation of olefins, whereas the Fe-porphyrinic MOFs were much less efficient. This was caused by the deactivation of Fe-porphyrinic MOFs by self-oxidation. $\mathbf{M}_{2}^{\prime}\mathbf{(HOOC)(Mn–por{-}2)}$ didn’t display size-selectivity in catalysis, since the inner Mn centers were coordinated by format pillars and inaccessible to the substrates. On the other hand, $\mathbf{M_{2}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{HOOC})(\mathbf{Fe}\mathbf{-por}\mathbf{-}2)$ still exhibited good performances in intermolecular aldol reaction of aldehydes and ketones. In both catalytic systems, the porphyrinic MOF catalysts displayed better activities than the corresponding homogeneous $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{por}{-}2)$ catalysts.
Another porous Mn-porphyrinic MOF of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{MnOH{-}p o r{-}}2)$ - (dpni) $\mathrm{(dpni}=N,N\mathrm{-}$ -di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide) was reported to possess channels with the size of $6.1\times7.8\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ .5 $^{7d}\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{MnOH-por-2})(\mathrm{dpni})$ can effectively catalyze the epoxidation of olefins in a heterogeneous manner with sizeselectivity. The conversion of styrene in the presence of this Mn-porphyric MOF could be as high as $99\%$ . Adsorption experiments also exhibited size-selectivity, and $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{MnOH}\cdot$ por-2)(dpni) took up 2.5 molecules of styrene, 4.0 molecules of cyclohexene and 2.0 molecules of cyclohexane per formula unit. The crystal structure of the styrene-included $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{MnOH}-$ por-2)(dpni) $\supset2$ styrene was obtained. These results suggest that the active catalytic sites should be Mn-por-2 moieties within the pores of the framework.
Zhou developed isostructural MOFs $\mathbf{Zr}_{3}(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{\cdotpor}\mathbf{-}2)$ $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}\right.$ , ${\bf C o}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+})$ (denoted as PCN-222), which were produced by solvothermal reactions of M-por-2, $\mathrm{zrCl_{4}}$ and benzoic acid in DMF or DEF at $120~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ The frameworks consisted of $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters linked by M-por-2 ligands. Different from the 12-connected $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster in the UiO-series of MOFs, the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster was connected to eight porphyrin ligands. PCN-222 contained large 1D open channels with a diameter of up to $3.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption experiment of PCN-222 at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ disclosed the typical type IV isotherm, confirming the MOFs with mesopores. In addition, a BET surface area of $2200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was determined through the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption. Among these Zr-MOFs, PCN-222(Fe) was exceptionally stable, and can resist in concentrated HCl acid. Catalytic studies showed that PCN-222(Fe) can promote the oxidation of pyrogallol, 3,3,5,5-tetramethylbenzidine and $o$ -phenylenediamine with both excellent substrate binding affinity and catalytic activity. These oxidation reactions are often used to characterize the catalytic performance of heme-like enzyme mimics.
This group further developed a series of highly stable MOFs with the formula of $\mathrm{Zr}_{4}(\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{por}{-}2)$ (denoted as PCN-224; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{n}\mathbf{o}$ metal, Ni, Co, Fe) with 3D nanochannels $\left(1.9\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ .58b The structural difference of PCN-222 and PCN-224 lies in the 8-connected $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster in the former and 6-connected $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in the latter. The porosity of PCN-224 was examined by $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ adsorption at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ which exhibited a typical type-I isotherm. Based on the ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake, the BET surface area was calculated to be $2600\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . PCN-224 possesses very high chemical stability, and the framework is retained in the wide pH range from 0 to 11. PCN-224(Co) was employed in the cycloaddition reactions of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and epoxides for three consecutive runs. Results showed that even in the third run, the TON and TOF were 518 and $129{\mathrm{~h}}^{-1}$ , respectively, suggesting that PCN224(Co) was a reusable heterogeneous catalyst for the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/$ epoxide cycloaddition reactions (Fig. 31).
Besides the aforementioned porphyrin-walled MOFs, Eddaoudi et al.58c and Zaworotko et al.58d have designed and synthesized porphyrin-encapsulated MOFs (porph@MOF) from MOFs containing polyhedral cages, which possess several features such as large cavities and good stability. These porph@MOF materials have shown good catalytic ability in hydrocarbon oxidations.
## 3.2 Metallosalen-based MOFs
By employing a dipyridyl MnIII(salen-1)Cl (salen- $\cdot1=(R,R)–(-)–1,2–$ cyclohexanediamino- $\cdot N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-tert-butyl-5-(4-pyridyl)salicylidene)) catalyst as a strut in combination with biphenyldicarboxylate (bpdc) or tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (tcpb), Hupp’s group prepared two zinc paddlewheel MOFs including the catenated $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}$ (Mn-salen-1) and noncatenated $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(t c p b)(M n{\cdot}s a l e n{-}1)}.$ .59 MOF $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(t c p b)(M n{\cdot}s a l e n{\cdot}1)}$ consists of a single network with unobstructed channels $(a~\times~b~=~22.4~\times~11.7~\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2})^{59b}$ in comparison to the catenated MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{Mn}\mathrm{-salen}{-1})$ 1 $(15.7\mathring{\mathbf{A}}\times$ $6.2\ \mathring\mathrm{A}^{2}\ .$ .59a In both frameworks, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ sites were accessible to the channels. Their catalytic activities toward asymmetric epoxidation were examined with 2,2-dimethyl- $2H\cdot$ -chromene as the substrate and 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl)iodosylbenzene as the oxidant (Fig. 32). The epoxide product was obtained with $88\%$ and $80\%$ ee in the presence of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}$ (Mn-salen-1) and $\mathrm{{Zn}_{2}(t c p b)}$ (Mn-salen-1), respectively. The observed enantiomeric excesses (ee) rival those of the free molecular catalyst $(\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ - (salen-1)Cl, $90\%$ ee) under the same reaction conditions. To determine whether catalysis by $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}$ (Mn-salen-1) occurs predominantly within the channel or instead on the external surface, competitive size selectivity studies were performed. A large porphyrinic substrate, (5,10,15,20-tetraphenylporphyrinato)- $\mathrm{tin}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ bis(4-vinyl)benzoate, and a small substrate, ethyl 4-vinyl benzoate, were mixed in a 1 : 1 olefinic unit ratio and reacted with 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl) iodosylbenzene in the presence of the MOF catalyst. The ratio of small to large substrate reactivity increased from 2 to 18 at $45\%$ conversion. In comparison, the homogeneous catalyst MnIII(salen-1)Cl gave a time-independent 1 : 1 reactivity ratio. The size-selectivity suggested that the MOFcatalyzed reactions mostly took place in the channels.
A series of isoreticular chiral MOFs (CMOFs) of tunable open channel sizes have been constructed by Lin’s group, which were assembled from $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\upmu_{4}{\cdot}\mathbf{O})(\mathbf{O}_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{R})_{6}$ SBUs and Mn-salen-derived dicarboxylic acids composed of the same central unit $^{(R,R)}$ -1,2- cyclohexanediamino- $\cdot N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-tert-butyl-salicylidene) $){\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{III}}{\bf C}{\bf l}$ and different terminal groups in the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -positions (–COOH for salen-2, $\scriptstyle-\mathbf{CH}=\mathbf{CHCOOH}$ for salen-3, $\mathbf{-C_{6}H_{4}C O O H}$ for salen-4, and $\scriptstyle-\mathbf{CH}=\mathbf{CHC}_{6}\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{COOH}$ for salen-5).60 Reactions of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (salen-2)Cl and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}(\mathrm{salen}{-3})\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ in DMF/EtOH led to the formation of two-fold interpenetrated structures of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{Mn}\cdot$ salen-2) $\mathbf{\tau}_{3}]{\cdot}20\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{F}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (CMOF-1) and $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}O)(M n{\cdot}s a l e n{\cdot}3)_{3}]}}.$ 42DMF (CMOF-2), respectively.60a In contrast, the similar reactions in DEF/EtOH gave rise to the non-interpenetrated frameworks of $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}-O)(M n-s a l e n-2)_{3}]cdot22D E F cdot4H_{2}}}$ (CMOF-3) and $[\mathrm{Zn_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})\cdot}$ (Mn- $\mathrm{salen{-}3)_{3}}\mathrm{\overline{{1}}\mathrm{.37DEF{\cdot}23E t O H{\cdot}4H_{2}O}}$ (CMOF-4). Interestingly, no matter what solvent systems were used, a three-fold interpenetrated framework of $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}O)(M n{\cdot}s a l e n{-}4)_{3}]}}.$ 38DMF11EtOH (CMOF-5) could be obtained by the reaction of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{salen}{-4})\mathbf{Cl}.$ The largest dimensions of open channels of these CMOFs were $8\times6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ (CMOF-1), $15\times7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ (CMOF-2), $20\times$ 16 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ (CMOF-3), $25\times23\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ (CMOF-4), and $11\times8\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ (CMOF-5), respectively. Catalytic activities of CMOFs 1-5 toward asymmetric epoxidation of unfunctionalized olefins were evaluated with 2,2- dimethyl-2H-chromene as the substrate and 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl) iodosylbenzene as the oxidant, and the conversion rate increases in the order of CMOF- $\mathrm{\cdot1<CMOF^{-}5<C M O F^{-}2<C M O F^{-}3<C M O F^{-}4.}$ In addition, CMOFs 4 and 5 are highly effective catalysts for asymmetric epoxidation of chromene to generate (1R,2S)-indene oxide in $85\%$ ee and $92\%$ ee, respectively (Fig. 33). On the other hand, reactions of $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (salen-5)Cl in DBF/EtOH (DBF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dibutylformamide) yielded $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}O)(M n{\cdot}s a l e n{-}5)_{3}]}}$ 40DBF 6EtOH $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (CMOF-6), whose open channels of triangular shapes with the edge length of $29\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ ran along the $\left\langle001\right\rangle$ and $\left\langle1-1-1\right\rangle$ directions.60b In the presence of CMOF-6, $\scriptstyle(1R,2S)$ - indene oxide was obtained with $84\%$ ee.
This group also developed ${\mathrm{Ru}}({\mathrm{salen}})$ -based MOFs, the twofold interpenetrated CMOF-7 with the formula of $\scriptstyle\{{\mathrm{Zn}}_{4}(\upmu_{4}-\mathrm{O})+$ $[\mathrm{Ru^{III}(s a l e n–2)(p y)_{2}C l]_{3}}\}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DBF})_{7}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DEF})_{7}$ , and the non-interpenetrated CMOF-8 with the formula of $\mathrm{\{Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}O)[R u^{I I I}(s a l e n{\cdot}2)(p y)_{2}C l]_{3}\}}.$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\bigl(\mathrm{DEF}\bigr)_{19}\cdot\bigl(\mathrm{DMF}\bigr)_{5}\cdot\bigl(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\bigr)_{17}}}\end{array}$ , which were prepared by solvothermal reactions of $[\mathrm{Ru(salen-2)(py)_{2}}]\mathrm{Cl}$ with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DBF/ DEF/EtOH and in DEF/DMF/EtOH, respectively.60c The largest cavities in the interpenetrated networks of CMOF-7 were measured to be $8\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ in diameter and were connected to each other by $4\times3\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ windows, whereas CMOF-8 showed much larger open channel dimensions of $14\times10\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ and a cavity size of $17\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ . Treatment of CMOF-7 and CMOF-8 with strong reducing agents such as $\mathrm{LiBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ (superhydride) or $\mathbf{NaB(OMe)}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ led to CMOF- ${\cdot}{7}^{\prime}$ and CMOF- $8^{\prime}$ in which the $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ centers have been reduced to $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers. The CMOF- $\cdot8^{\prime}$ -catalyzed asymmetric cyclopropanation of styrene was obtained with very high diastereo- and enantioselectivities $\mathrm{(d.r.~=~7:1~}$ (trans/cis); ee $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $91\%$ (trans) and $84\%$ (cis)) in the presence of $\mathbf{NaBH(OMe)}_{3}$ . In contrast, a similar cyclopropanation reaction with CMOF- ${\cdot}{7}^{\prime}$ as the catalyst gave the desired products in less than $1\%$ yield and with modest ee values ( $33\%$ (trans) and $47\%$ (cis)). This might be due to the reduced open channels in CMOF- ${\cdot}{7}^{\prime}$ relative to those in CMOF- $8^{\prime}$ , thus preventing the diffusion of the reagents into the $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}^{\mathrm{II}},$ salen active sites in the interior of CMOF- ${\cdot}{7}^{\prime}$ .
Similarly, the two-fold interpenetrated MOF $\{\mathbf{Zn_{4}}(\upmu_{4}\mathbf{-O})\cdot$ 一 $[\mathrm{Ru^{\mathrm{III}}(s a l e n–3)(p y)_{2}C l l_{3}}\}_{2}\cdot(\mathrm{DBF})_{10}\cdot(\mathrm{DEF})_{7}$ (CMOF-9) and noninterpenetrated $\{\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\upmu_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{salen{-}}3)(\mathrm{py})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}]_{3}\}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DEF})_{51}$ (CMOF-10) were obtained by the solvothermal reactions in DBF/DEF/EtOH and in DEF/DMF/EtOH, respectively, when the salen-3 ligand was used instead.60d CMOF-9 and CMOF-10 possessed 3D inter-connected channels, with the sizes of $7\times$ $7\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ and $19\times19\stackrel{\circ}{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , respectively. The reduced form CMOF- $10^{\prime}$ displayed better catalytic performances in the cyclopropanation of olefins than CMOF- $9^{\prime}$ .
More recently, Cui’s group developed a Co(salen)-based MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{4}(C o(s a l e n-2))_{4}(D M F)_{4}(O A c)_{4}]\cdot4H_{2}O_{3}}}$ , which was synthesized by heating a 2 : 1 mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Co(salen-2)(OAc) in DMF and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ .61a MOF ${\bf C d}_{4}({\bf C o}(\mathrm{salen-}2))_{4}.$ $(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}$ adopted a 3D chiral nanoporous framework that was built from $\mathrm{Cd}_{4}$ clusters and Co(salen-2) linkers with open channels of $\sim12\times8\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $a$ -axis. The Co(salen)-based framework was shown to be an efficient and recyclable heterogeneous catalyst for hydrolytic kinetic resolution (HKR) of a range of benzyloxy epoxide derivatives bearing both electrondonating and electron-withdrawing substituents with up to $99.5\%$ ee (Fig. 34). It was noted that the examined epoxides exhibited good to excellent kinetic resolution selectivities $\left(K_{\mathrm{rel}}=\right.$ 13–43, the ratio of relative rates of the two substrate enantiomers). This Co(salen) MOF exhibited size-selective catalysis. While the reaction with the small substrate phenoxy epoxide led to $56\%$ conversion, the one with the sterically more demanding substrate triphenyl glycidyl ether gave only less than $5\%$ conversion under the same reaction conditions. This result suggested that this bulky substrate cannot access the catalytic sites in the porous structure due to its large diameter, and catalytic HKR should occur within the MOF.
In addition to these porous metallosalenic frameworks, Cao’s group reported 1D homochiral Ni-salen-1 based coordination polymers, which performed as self-supported heterogeneous catalysts.61b
In summary, only five achiral porphyrin ligands have been used so far to construct MOFs, and most of the catalytic applications of these porphyrinic MOFs are related to the oxidation reactions of olefins either of epoxidation or transformations to carbonyl-functionalized substrates. This discipline is rather immature compared to homogeneous metal–porphyrin catalysts, and thus it is in high need to develop more catalytical porphyrinic MOFs, especially containing chiral porphyrin ligands. In comparison, MOFs derived from metallosalen ligands showed richer catalytic chemistry. It might be because the chiral adduct is easier to be induced to the metallosalen ligand than the metalloporphyrin ligand. Chiral salen ligands can be readily prepared by the condensation of aryl aldehyde and commercial available (1R,2R)- $(-)\cdot$ diaminocyclohexane. As a result, salen-containing MOFs have been applied in asymmetric catalysis such as epoxidation, cyclopropanation and hydrolytic kinetic resolution with excellent ee values.
## 4. MOFs with functional organic sites
As discussed above, studies on the catalytic performance of MOFs with the metal sites taking the essential role in catalysis have been thoroughly studied. However, only a few proofs of concept with regard to the catalytically-active functional organic sites (FOS) in MOF catalysis have been reported.62–81
Concerning base-catalyzed reactions, $\mathrm{Kim},^{62}$ Kitagawa,63 and Gascon64a have demonstrated the possibility to use as-synthesized MOFs as basic catalysts by introducing pyridyl, amide and amino groups, respectively, in the organic linkers. In addition,
Chang grafted amine onto the unsaturated metal sites of MOFs.65 These base-type catalysts have been employed in the Knoevenagel condensation and transesterification reactions. Among these areas, the introduction of an amino-group into the MOFs has been particularly effective. Through covalent postsynthesis, Schiff base-containing MOFs can be formed due to the reaction of the amino group and an added aldehyde/ketone reagent, which in turn undergoes complexation.66–70
In respect of acid-catalyzed reactions, the sulfoxy group $\left(-\mathrm{{SO}_{3}H}\right)$ has also been introduced through the aromatic linker of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-101(Cr) by Gascon et al.71 and Zhu et al.,72 respectively. These acid catalysts have been employed in the esterification of carboxylic acids and alcohols.
As for fine reactions giving rise to more valuable products, organocatalysts such as pyrrolidine, N-heterocyclic carbene, urea, binol, bipyridine and catechol have been successfully incorporated into MOFs.73–81 Kim,73 Telfer74 and Duan75 have prepared pyrrolidine-based MOFs, which can effectively promote the aldol reactions with modest to good ee values. Hupp and the co-workers developed NHC-/urea-based MOFs, which can catalyze the addition of alcohols to $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketones and Friedel–Crafts reaction, respectively.76,78 Wu’s group immobilized Pd onto the NHC-containing MOFs, and the obtained Pd-NHC MOFs effectively promoted the Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling reaction of aryl halides with phenylboronic acids.77 Lin’s group designed a series of binol-containing ligands to construct functional MOFs, and the latter reacted with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ complexes, yielding Ti-binolate MOFs that can perform as active catalysts in the $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ addition reactions with aromatic aldehydes and show excellent ee values (up to $93\%$ ).79 Li et al. firstly exploited the catalytic activity of the uncoordinated $^{2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ 一 bipyridine in MOF-253 in cross-coupling of aryl halides with inactivated arenes, and further prepared MOF-253 CuI through the post-synthetic method and studied its application in the C–O cross-coupling of phenols and alcohols with aryl halides.80 Hupp et al. grafted catechol to MIL-101(Cr) and then immobilized $\mathbf{V}^{5^{+}}$ through complexation.81
## 4.1 Pyridyl and amide groups
As one of the pioneering studies in MOF catalysis, in as early as 2000, Kim et al. studied the asymmetric catalysis of MOFs with the pyridyl group behaving as the functional organic site, although only low enantioselectivity was achieved with an enantiomeric excess value of $8\%$ . They reported the synthesis of a homochiral MOF, D-POST-1 with the formula of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})]\cdot$ - (dpdc)6] ${\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{O}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}}$ (Hdpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ (4S,5S)-2,2-dimethyl-5-[(4-pyridinylamino)carbonyl]-1,3-dioxolane-4-carboxylic acid).62 The structure of D-POST-1 shows the existence of large chiral 1D channels, the cross-section of which was best described as an equilateral triangle with a side length of $13.4\ \mathring\mathrm{A}.$ A half of pyridine groups extrude into the channel without any interactions with the framework, as illustrated in Fig. 35. These exposed pyridyl groups provide the MOF with unique opportunities in heterogeneous catalysis. The catalytic activity of D-POST-1 was examined in the transesterification of 2,4-dinitrophenyl acetate. Treatment of 2,4-dinitrophenyl acetate and ethanol with suspension of 0.1 equivalent of the MOF catalyst in carbon tetrachloride for $55\mathrm{~h~}$ at $27\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ produced ethyl acetate in $77\%$ yield. However, transesterification of 2,4-dinitrophenyl acetate with bulkier alcohols, for example, isobutanol, neopentanol and 3,3,3-triphenyl-1- propanol, takes place with a much slower or even negligible rate under the same reaction conditions. This size selective observation suggests that the catalysis mainly occurs in the channels. Moreover, POST-1 manifested enantioselective catalytic activity for the transesterification of 2,4-dinitrophenyl acetate. By using D-POST-1 or $\mathbf{L}$ -POST-1 as catalysts, $s$ or $R$ enantiomeric ester products were obtained with about $8\%$ enantiomeric excess. In spite of the modest enantioselectivity, such an asymmetric catalytic process in MOF pores is believed noteworthy because asymmetric induction has never been observed in reactions mediated by modular porous materials.
Kitagawa’s group for the first time employed a threeconnector ligand 4-btapa $(N^{1},N^{3},N^{5}$ -tri(pyridin-4-yl)benzene1,3,5-tricarboxamide) containing three pyridyl groups as coordination sites and three amide groups as guest interaction sites in the self-assembly of a base-type catalytic porous framework, $\{\mathrm{[Cd(4\mathrm{-}b t a p a)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}]\cdot6H_{2}O\cdot2D M F}\}_{n}$ (Cd-btapa) (Fig. 36).63 The coordination framework was constructed from the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ center as a six-connected node using 4-btapa as a threeconnected linker, producing three-dimensionally running channels with dimensions of $4.7\times7.3\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ . The zigzag channels were occupied by nitrate anions and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and DMF molecules. Importantly, the amide groups are highly ordered on the surfaces of the channels, offering a chance to interact with guest molecules. The TGA data indicated that Cd-btapa was stable up to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Taking advantage of these structural characters, they studied the Lewis-base catalysis of Cd-btapa, and examined its catalytic activity in Knoevenagel condensation reactions of benzaldehyde with active methylene compounds, such as malononitrile, ethyl cyanoacetate, and cyano-acetic acid tert-butyl ester. It was found that the malononitrile was a good substrate, producing $98\%$ conversion of the adduct, whereas other substrates with larger sizes reacted negligibly. The size-selectivity suggested that the catalytic reactions should occur within the channels. The heterogeneity and recyclability of solid Cd-btapa were examined through filtration and recycling experiments, confirmed by the powder XRD patterns of the catalyst before and after the reactions for high stability of the heterogeneous catalyst. This is the first report on the use of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts for the Knoevenagel condensation. After this,
Knoevenagel condensation has gradually become a benchmark reaction for the test of the Lewis base catalysis of MOFs.
## 4.2 Amino and aldehyde groups
Gascon groups showed their interest in the study of the application of amino-functionalized MOFs in Lewis base catalysis by developing an amino-containing MOF, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Al), and observed excellent activities and selectivity of these MOFs to the Knoevenagel condensation.64a These amino-containing MOFs were stable under the studied reaction conditions, and could be reused without significant loss in activity. More recently, this group developed an oxamate-functionalized MOF, MIL-101(Cr)-PMA (PMA refers to phenylene-mono(oxanate)), which was prepared by the reaction of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Cr) and ethyl chlorooxoacetate at $343\ \mathrm{K}.^{64b}$ Cu complexes can be incorporated into this oxamate-containing MOF. Two Cu precursors, Cu nitrate and Cu ethylenediamine, have been employed, leading to the formation of MIL-101(Cr)-PMA-Cu and MIL-101(Cr)- PMA-CuEDA, respectively. ICP-OES analysis revealed that the Cu contents were about $9\mathrm{wt\%}$ in both cases. The Cu-MOFs exhibit good catalytic performances in 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition reactions of azides and alkynes as well as multicomponent coupling reactions (propargylamine formation).
Another example of an amine-functionalized MOF, ED-MIL101(Cr) $\mathbf{\DeltaED=}$ ethylenediamine), was obtained by coordination of ED to the dehydrated MIL-101 framework in Chang’s group.65 The synthesis of ED-MIL-101(Cr) was achieved by refluxing ED and the dehydrated MIL-101(Cr) framework in toluene. The almost unchanged X-ray diffraction patterns confirmed that the ED grafting occurs with no apparent loss of crystallinity, but with some slight variations of the Bragg intensities. The catalytic performance of dehydrated ED-MIL101(Cr) was measured by using the Knoevenagel condensation as a model reaction. In the presence of a catalytic amount of ED-MIL-101(Cr), the condensation of benzaldehyde into transethyl cyanocinnamate was accomplished with $97.1\%$ yield and $99.1\%$ trans selectivity. Moreover, in terms of turnover frequency (TOF), ED-MIL-101(Cr) showed a remarkably superior activity $\left(\mathrm{TOF}=328\mathrm{h}^{-1}\right).$ . The recyclability test of ED-MIL-101(Cr) clearly showed that it was easily isolated from the reaction suspension by filtration and can be reused without significant loss of activity in the third run.
Yaghi et al. developed IRMOF-3 (isoreticular MOF-3, $\mathrm{Zn_{4}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2})_{3}]$ with a free diameter of 9.6 Å decorated with pendant amine $\left(-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ groups, whose crystalline structure is similar to the well-known MOF-5 (or IRMOF-1).66a The amine moieties are not intimately involved in the framework backbone, and therefore are available to undergo chemical transformations (Fig. 37).66–68 A desirable amine transformation via a simple condensation reaction was reported by Rosseinsky et al., producing a salicylidene (sal) moiety IRMOF-3-sal which subsequently underwent metal binding with ${\bf V}(\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{acac})_{2}{\cdot}{\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}$ (acac $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ acetylacetonate) to lead to the formation of IRMOF-3-sal-V (Fig. 37).66b Comparable powder XRD patterns for IRMOF-3-sal and IRMOF-3- sal-V with IRMOF-3 showed the persistence of the extended open framework structure. IRMOF-3-sal-V was found to be active for the oxidation of cyclohexene with TBHP ( $40\%$ conversion of cyclohexene in THF at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{{h}}}$ ). Catalytic behavior was demonstrated to be heterogeneous through the filtration experiment. However, the poor turnover frequencies combined with loss of framework integrity after catalytic reactions limited its further applicability.
Concurrently, Corma employed a similar covalent postsynthesis methodology to prepare IRMOF-3 containing an $\mathrm{{Au}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{III}}})}$ Schiff base complex.67 IRMOF-3-sal-Au was prepared through reacting a suitable gold precursor, $\mathrm{\DeltaNaAuCl_{4}}$ , with IRMOF-3-sal. According to the elemental analysis of the samples, a maximum functionalization of about $3\%$ of the total $\boldsymbol{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ groups was accomplished without losing the framework integrity, while gold complexation by the Schiff base complex was almost quantitative. A $3\%$ functionalization of the Schiff base allowed introducing up to $2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of gold in the material. Powder XRD patterns of IRMOF-3-sal-Au remained unaltered when the material was heated up to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air, demonstrating that the PSM-MOF is thermally stable. IRMOF-3-SI-Au was then employed to catalyze the multicomponent domino coupling reaction of $N_{\mathbf{\lambda}}$ -tosyl protected ethynylaniline, paraformaldehyde and piperidine (Fig. 37). It was found that the conversion of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -tosyl protected ethynylaniline increased continuously up to $93\%$ over IRMOF-3-sal-Au within $^{14\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The IRMOF-3-sal-Au exhibited a large substrate scope relative to $N\mathrm{.}$ -protected ethylanilines, aldehydes and amines. Additionally, cyclohexylcarboxyaldehyde and octylaldehyde can also take part in this catalysis. The ethynylaniline bearing either an electron-donating methyl group or an electron-withdrawing methoxycarbonyl group performed well in the reactions. Remarkably, the reactions were also successful with chiral amines such as (s)- $\cdot(+)-2$ -(methoxymethyl)- pyrrolidine and (s)- $\cdot(+)-2$ -methylpiperdine, giving yields of up to $92\%$ .
Ahn et al. prepared an Mn-immobilized MOF via the binding of a manganese(II) acetylacetonate complex to IRMOF-3 through post-synthetic modification, which was accomplished by dissolving $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{acac})_{2}$ in toluene and treating the resulting solution with IRMOF-3 crystals at $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ .68 The obtained IRMOF3-Mn was stable in an inert atmosphere or in toluene for several months. The powder XRD patterns comparison between IRMOF-3 and IRMOF-3-Mn indicated that the original MOF porous structure was maintained after the post-synthetic reaction. Furthermore, no changes in the type $\mathbf{I}\ \mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were observed after post-modification of IRMOF-3, again suggesting that the structure of the parent material remained intact after functionalization. Integrating $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{acac})_{2}$ into the MOF framework has been verified by means of FT-IR, ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR and XPS studies. ICP-MS analysis and elemental analysis of IRMOF-3-Mn revealed that the extent of binding of the Mn complex led to a final weight loading of $1.25\%$ Mn in the MOFs. The catalytic activity of IRMOF-3-Mn was tested for the epoxidation of alkenes using molecular oxygen and trimethylacetaldehyde as the oxidant (Fig. 37). The epoxidation of cyclohexene over IRMOF-3-Mn at $40~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ formed cyclohexene oxide with excellent selectivity $(92\%)$ and conversion $(68\%)$ . It was found that the larger molecule cyclooctene was converted to cyclooctene oxide with lower conversion $(60\%)$ and slightly higher selectivity $(96\%)$ than cyclohexene under the resembling reaction conditions. The reactivity of the double bond attached to benzylic rings displayed lower conversion and selectivity to epoxide than in the other two cyclic alkenes, cyclohexene and cyclooctene. At $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , styrene was converted to the corresponding epoxide with $52\%$ conversion and $81\%$ selectivity. Recycling experiments confirmed that conversion and selectivity to epoxide were almost identical irrespective of the number of cycles performed. Further confirmation of the stability of Mn in MOF was made through a hot filtering experiment. No Mn in the filtrate after the reaction was detected by ICP measurements. The powder XRD patterns and FTIR spectra of the solid catalyst after reuse were also indistinguishable from those of the fresh catalyst, indicating that no structural deterioration or organic and metal complex decomposition occurred after catalytic reactions.
As mentioned above, the UiO-66 MOF $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b d c)_{6}}$ shows high thermal stability due to the presence of an inner $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ core in which the triangular faces of the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ - octahedron are alternatively capped with $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{O}$ and $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups. Cohen and Lillerud independently discovered that the UiO-66 motif was very tolerant to functionalized ligands, allowing the synthesis of the amino-functionalized UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ with the same topology.69a,b Due to the presence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups in the linker, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 can be further metallated.
A PSM-MOF of UiO-66, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66-damp-Ir $\left({\mathrm{damp}}=6\cdot$ ((diisopropylamino)methyl)picolinaldehyde) was prepared by the reaction between the amino group of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and damp by Iglesias and Corma et al., leading to the formation of an imino-pincer complex, which further reacted with $\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{\mathrm{I}})$ complex $[\mathrm{IrCl(COD)}]_{2}$ .69c ICP-AES analysis of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66-damp-Ir indicated that the amount of iridium was $3.11~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Catalytic evaluation of the $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66-damp-Ir was performed by mixing them with the nitroarenes and carbonyl compounds in isopropanol in a batch reactor. Under the optimized reaction conditions, various aldehydes were reductively aminated with nitrobenzene under 6 bar pressure of molecular hydrogen. Hot filtration tests for $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66-damp-Ir proved that the catalytic activity was associated with the solid exclusively, and no reaction occurred when the catalyst was removed from the reaction mixture. MOF $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - UiO-66-damp-Ir can be recovered by simple filtration in air and reused without noticeable loss of catalytic activity for at least four cycles. The overall framework structure of the material remained intact: the powder XRD patterns and SEM images of both the fresh and the recovered catalysts after catalytic reactions matched very well. TEM images indicated that there was no formation of nanoparticles during the catalytic reactions.
The $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Fe})$ , which is isostructural to the MIL101(Cr), is formed by trimeric iron(III) octahedral clusters via linkage of 2-aminoterephthalate ligands.70a Canivet et al. reported a PSM-MOF, $\mathrm{Ni@NH_{2}{\cdot}M I L{\cdot}101(F e)}.$ , which was prepared via the imine condensation reaction between formyl groups of $\mathrm{\DeltaNi(PyCHO)Cl_{2}}$ and amine groups of ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101$ (Fe).70b The condensation yielded $10\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Fe})$ when 1 equiv. of $\mathrm{PyCHO/NiCl_{2}}$ per $\boldsymbol{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ moiety on the MOF was used, whereas $30\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL101(Fe) could be obtained when using 3 equiv. of $\mathrm{PyCHO/NiCl}_{2}$ . Powder XRD analysis confirmed the structural integrity of the solid materials after the functionalization step. As exemplified in Fig. 38, the catalytic activities of Ni@MIL-101(Fe) were examined using the liquid-phase ethylene dimerization as the benchmark reaction. Typically, $\mathrm{Ni@NH_{2}{\cdot}M I L{-}101(F e)}$ suspended in heptane was allowed to react in the presence of $\mathbf{Et}_{2}\mathbf{AlCl}$ under 15 bar of ethylene. The Ni@MIL-101(Fe) catalyst was rather active with turnover frequencies up to $3215~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ at $10\ {^\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , and the selectivity for 1-butene product was $94\%$ . The leaching test confirmed that no reaction occurred if the filtrated catalytic solution was again put under catalytic conditions in the presence of an Al-based co-catalyst. Powder XRD patterns of
10Ni@MIL-101(Fe) after a catalytic run verified that its crystallinity was kept. Furthermore, after careful washings with anhydrous ethanol and drying, the $10\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101\big(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathcal{A}}$ ) can be reused for at least two more catalytic runs without significant loss of activity or selectivity.
Excellent studies demonstrating MOF catalysis in a supramolecular way by using aldehyde-containing MOFs have been done by Farrusseng’s group.70c,d They functionalized ZIF-type materials to get SIM-1 (Substituted Imidazolate Material), which is isostructural to commercialized ZIF-8 under the name Basolite Z-1200. After grafting $\mathbf{C}_{12}$ alkyl chains onto the framework, they are able to efficiently control the hydrophilic-hydrophobic balance of the post-modified material, which drives the competitive adsorption of reactants and products.70c The introduction of alkyl chains was accomplished by the reaction of the aldehyde group of SIM-1 with dodecylamine. SIM-1 was firstly supported on $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -alumina beads, leading to SIM-1/alumina composites, which were further immersed in a solution of dodecylamine leading to the formation of eggshell SIM- $2\mathrm{(C_{12})}$ bead composites. Knoevenagel condensation was used to test the catalytic activity of the SIM on alumina beads. Catalytic results indicated that SIM-2(C12)/beads were 3-fold more efficient than SIM-1/beads due to the creation of a hydrophobic environment surrounding the catalytic sites in SIM-2(C12). Interestingly, it was shown that the contact angle of a catalyst thin layer with water can be directly correlated to the catalytic activity, taking advantage of water that could be produced by Knoevenagel condensation.
## 4.3 Sulfoxy group
A strategy to post-synthetically incorporate sulfoxy moieties into MIL-53(Al) was reported by Gascon’s group.71 Characterization results demonstrated that, by treating MIL-53(Al) with a mixture of sulfuric acid and triflic anhydride, sulfoxy acid moieties are covalently bonded to the aryl carbons of the organic linkers (Fig. 39). The acid catalytic property of the resulting functionalized MOF (denoted as S-MIL-53(Al)) was examined using the esterification of a 1 : 1 molar ratio of acetic acid and $n$ -butanol at $343\mathrm{~K~}$ . Due to adsorbed $\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ that were very difficult to eliminate except under reaction conditions, S-MIL-53(Al)
showed a high catalytic activity in the first run. Hence, the performance was related to the second use of the catalysts in the case of the sulfated MOFs. In the second run, the TOF for S-MIL-53(Al) was around $1\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Catalytic experiments with MIL-53(Al) showed the same conversion profile as the blank run, demonstrating that the catalysis can be attributed to the presence of sulfoxy acid groups within the framework pores. Functionalized S-MIL-53(Al) can be recycled without significant loss of activity. This TOF was maintained even after 5 reuse of the S-MIL-53(Al) catalyst.
On the other hand, a one-pot synthesis of the sulfoxy or sulfonic acid-functionalized MIL-101(Cr) MOF catalyst was presented by Zhang and Zhu et al. The functionalized S-MIL101(Cr) was obtained by the hydrothermal reaction of $\mathbf{CrO}_{3}$ , the 2-sulfoterephthalic acid monosodium salt and hydrochloric acid (12 M) at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 d.72 The catalytic activity of S-MIL101(Cr) was tested using the liquid-phase esterification of $n$ -hexanol with acetic acid. Over S-MIL-101(Cr), the yield of hexyl acetate was $65.1\%$ . For comparison, MIL-101(Cr) itself did not have any catalytic activity in the esterification. Additionally, S-MIL-101(Cr) showed a much high TOF value of $2.78\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ in the esterification under the optimized conditions. As a heterogeneous catalyst, S-MIL-101(Cr) can be easily recovered after the reaction by filtration and can be reused for 5 runs without significant loss of catalytic activity or selectivity. The results from XRD characterization of the used S-MIL-101(Cr) catalyst after the 5th-run reaction were almost the same as those for the fresh S-MIL-101(Cr), confirming the stability of the prepared S-MIL-101(Cr) catalyst.
## 4.4 Pyrrolidine
Pyrrolidines as proline-derived compounds have been widely as organocatalysts, and can efficiently promote asymmetric aldol reactions. By postsynthetic modification with chiral ligands $(S)–N\cdot$ -(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide (3-ppc) and (S)- $N\cdot$ -(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide (4-ppc), Kim’s group reported the synthesis of chiral MOFs possessing the pyrrolidine functional group from a pre-assembled achiral MOF, MIL101(Cr).73 The chiral ligands can be coordinated to the open metal coordination sites of MIL-101(Cr), leading to the formation of $\left[\mathrm{Cr_{3}O(3–p p c)_{1.8}(H_{2}O)_{0.2}F(b d c)_{3}}\right]\cdot0.15\mathrm{(H_{2}b d c)}\cdot\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (CMIL-1)
and $\mathrm{\small{\left[Cr_{3}O(4-p p c)_{1.75}(H_{2}O)_{0.25}F(b d c)_{3}\right]}}.0.15\mathrm{(H_{2}b d c)}.\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (CMIL-2). Elemental analysis data indicated that $\sim1.8$ ligands per formula were incorporated for both CMILs. Nitrogen adsorption measurements indicated that both chiral MOFs were highly porous even after post modification (BET surface area, $1420\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CMIL-1 and $1375\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CMIL-2). The catalytic activities of CMILs were tested for asymmetric aldol reactions between various aromatic aldehydes and ketones (Fig. 40). The reactions catalyzed by CMILs produced aldol products in good to excellent yields $(60-90\%)$ with fair to good enantioselectivity for $R\mathrm{.}$ -isomers (ee $55\mathrm{-}80\%$ . Recycling experiment showed that CMIL-1 can be reused for the asymmetric aldol reaction between paranitrobenzaldehyde and acetone up to three times without a significant change in yield and enantioselectivity. A size selectivity study was performed to make sure that the aldol reactions occur mostly in the framework cavities of CMILs. While the reaction between bulky aldehyde 5-formyl-1,3-phenylene-bis-(3,5- di-tert-butylbenzoate), which was larger than the window size of CMIL-1, and para-nitrobenzaldehyde in the presence of 3-ppc was completed within $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ , only $5\%$ of the aldol product was observed in the presence of CMIL-1 after the same period of reaction time.
Telfer’s group also presented a strategy for incorporating pyrrolidine into MOFs,74 in which the functional MOFs were built on the fact that biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid ligands, including those substituted at the 2 and/or $2^{\prime}$ positions, can react with zinc(II) to form IRMOFs. They chose (S)-2-(1-(tertbutoxycarbonyl)pyrrolidine-2-carboxamido)- $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylic acid ((S)-pro-boc) as the building block. The solvothermal reaction of (S)-pro-boc with ${\bf\cal Z}{\bf n}({\bf N O}_{3})_{2}$ in $^{N,N-}$ diethylformamide (DEF) produced well-faceted, colorless, cubic crystals of IRMOF-pro-boc. The crystal structure closely resembles other members of the IRMOF series. IRMOF-pro-boc can be converted to IRMOF-pro in a single-crystal-to-single-crystal manner by heating to $165^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DMF under microwave irradiation (Fig. 41).
IRMOF-Pro was then employed as a heterogeneous catalyst in aldol reactions. Reaction of acetone with 4-nitrobenzaldehyde gave the aldol product 4-hydroxy-4-(4-nitrophenyl)-pentan-2-one with $29\%$ ee. The aldol reaction of cyclopentanone and 4-nitrobenzaldehyde to give hydroxy(4-nitrophenyl)methylcyclopentanone was also tested. The diastereomeric ratio (dr) of the anti to the syn products was determined to be 75 : 25, with ee’s of $14\%$ for the anti isomer and $3\%$ for the syn isomer, respectively.
Two homochiral MOFs of Cd-tbt and Zn-pyi containing pyrrolidine units were synthesized by Duan’s group.75 Cd-tbt was produced by a hydrothermal reaction using cadmium perchlorate, 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{tbt}\right)$ and L-N-tert-butoxy-carbonyl-2-(imidazole)-1-pyrrolidine (L-bcip) as the reactants.75a In the coordination framework of Cd-tbt, the rhombus channels with dimensions of $4.0~\times~6.5~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ were constructed through tumbling one 2D sheet to another in an interpenetration fashion, and molecules of L-pyrrolidine-2-ylimidazole (L-pyi) were located above or beneath the 2D interpenetration layer with the potential catalytic active sites $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ of pyrrolidine exposed (Fig. 42). The catalytic ability of Cd-tbt was tested using the asymmetric aldol reactions between aromatic aldehydes and cyclohexanone. The reaction with the heterogeneous catalyst Cd-tbt achieved $30\%$ higher ee than the one with the homogeneous catalyst L-pyi. The better catalytic performances of Cd-tbt might partially originate from the restricted movement of the substrate on the MOF’s exterior coupled with multiple chiral inductions, since the channel of Cd-tbt was not large enough to encapsulate molecules of L-pyi and the substrates.
On the other hand, the precursor Zn-bcip was formed by the solvothermal reaction of $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -tricarboxyltriphenylamine $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{tca}\right)$ and $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the presence of $_\mathrm{~L~}$ -bcip in a mixed solvent of DMF and ethanol.75b In the coordination framework of Zn-bcip, 1D channels of $12\times16\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ were formed along the [100] direction with $\mathrm{~L~}$ -bcip molecules located within the channels. Zn-pyi was then obtained after heating Zn-bcip in DMF at $165~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using microwave irradiation for about $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Zn-pyi was employed to prompt the asymmetric $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ -alkylation of aliphatic aldehydes (Fig. 42) using a common fluorescent lamp (26 W) as the light source. Under the optimized conditions, the reaction between phenylpropylaldehyde and diethyl 2-bromomalonate finished with a high reaction efficiency $74\%$ in yield) and an excellent enantioselectivity ( $92\%$ ee). In the presence of bulky aldehyde 3-(3-oxopropyl)phenyl 3,5-di-tert-butylbenzoate, whose size $[17.4\times13.8\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}]$ was larger than the pore size of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pyi as the aldehyde substrate, the photocatalytic $\mathfrak{a}$ -alkylation reaction only gave $7\%$ of the product under the same reaction conditions. The size selectivity of the substrate suggested that the alkylation reactions occurred mostly in the channel of the catalyst, not on the external surface. Zn-pyi can be easily isolated from the reaction suspension by simple filtration alone and can be reused at least three times with a slight decrease in its reactivity and selectivity (yield decreasing from $74\%$ to $70\%$ with the ee value decreasing from $92\%$ to $88\%$ ).
## 4.5 N-Heterocyclic carbene
N-Heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) are most frequently prepared via deprotonation of the corresponding azolium salts such as imidazolium. NHCs are powerful organocatalysts, and will react with $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketones to form enote intermediates, which then take part in a broad range of transformations. On the other hand, NHCs are electron-rich, neutral $\upsigma$ -donor ligands, and can form very strong bonds with the majority of metals. And thus, NHCs have mostly been used as ancillary ligands for the preparation of transition metal-based catalysts. Incorporation of catalytically active NHCs in MOFs is an interesting topic.76 Farha, Scheidt and Hupp et al. reported their work about converting Willans’ tripodal imidazolate framework-1 (TIF-1, $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{Cl}_{3}(\mathrm{tmtb})_{2}$ , tmtb $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathbf{\Lambda}_{1,3,5}$ -trimethyl imidazole-2,4,6- triethyl benzene) to carbene-like species through deprotonation of coordinated imidazoles (Fig. 43).77a MOF TIF-1 possesses onedimensional channels with the pore diameter at its smallest measures $8.5\mathrm{\textrm{\AA}}$ TIF-1 displayed high thermal stability, and retained crystallinity after heating under vacuum at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for two days. One notable feature of TIF-1 was that the $\mathbf{C4}/\mathbf{C5}$ sites of the imidazoles, rather than C2 sites, protruded into the framework pores. The space-filling model of TIF-1 crystal structure indicated that the C2 carbon was completely inaccessible to atoms or molecules entering the pores. As a result, under special circumstances, NHCs were deprotonated at C4 or C5, forming abnormal, or, aNHCs within the pores of TIF-1. The catalytic activity of deprotonated TIF-1 was examined using the addition of alcohols to $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketones. In the presence of TIF-1 solids, the conjugate addition of methanol and benzyl alcohol to $(E)$ -hex-4-ene-3-one yielded the corresponding ethers with $65\%$ and $83\%$ conversion, respectively. Powder X-ray diffraction of TIF-1 before and after catalysis suggested retention of crystallinity. Competition reactions of a 1 : 1 molar ratio of benzyl alcohol and methanol were conducted to probe whether catalysis occurred primarily on the surface of the MOF or within the pores. No significant differences in reactivity were noted, suggesting that catalysis occurred chiefly on the surface of the MOF rather than inside the MOF pores.
NHCs can also be generated in Zwitterionic organic ligands. Wu et al. designed two imidazolium-based ligands, $^{1,1^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ methylenebis-(3-(4-carboxyphenyl)- $\cdot1H\cdot$ -imidazol-3-ium) (mcpi) and $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -methylenebis-(3-(4-carboxy-2-methylphenyl)- $\cdot1H\cdot$ -imidazol-3- ium) (mcpiMe). $^{77b,c}$ They used these two ligands to construct novel MOFs of $\mathrm{\Gamma[Cu_{2}(m c p i)_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]{\cdot}4N O_{3}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (Cu-mcpi) and $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(mcpiMe)Cl_{2}]\cdot8H_{2}O}}$ (Zn-mcpiMe). On one hand, Cu-mcpi was prepared by heating a mixture of $\mathbf{H}_{2}(\mathrm{mcpi})\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixed solvent of MeOH and EtOH at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for one day.77b Single-crystal structure analysis of Cu-mcpi revealed large cavities with the cross dimensions of $18\times34\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}^{2}$ . On the other hand, Zn-mcpiMe was synthesized by the reaction of $\mathbf{H}_{2}(\mathrm{mcpi}{\cdot}\mathbf{Me})\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixed solvent of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and EtOH at room temperature for three days (Fig. 44).77c Zn-mcpiMe had a very large exterior wall diameter of $4.91\mathrm{nm}$ and an interior channel diameter of $3.32~\mathrm{nm}$ . Interlocking of the nanotubes gave rise to a 3D chiral framework containing 1D helical cylinder-like channels with a diameter of $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . It is noted that the imidazolium moieties in both MOFs are readily available to be deprotonated for the formation of NHCs, which can bind with catalytic metal ions to form metallocarbenes (e.g. Pd-NHC). This can be conveniently performed by treating the MOFs with $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ in THF solvent. ICP-MS showed that around 11.0 and $7.8\%$ palladium could be immobilized in the porous frameworks of Cu-mcpi and ZnmcpiMe, respectively. The Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction of aryl halides with phenylboronic acids was carried out to evaluate the catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{{\bfPd}^{\mathrm{H}}}$ -immobilized porous materials (Fig. 44). Catalytic results showed that the PSM-MOFs (e.g. Pd/Cumcpi and Pd/Zn-mcpiMe) can catalyze the coupling reaction of a variety of phenylbromides or phenyliodides with phenylboronic acids in toluene at $70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the presence of $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ . The hot filtration experiments showed that no reaction occurred in the absence of the solid catalyst. The $\mathbf{Pd}^{\mathrm{{II}}}.$ -immobilized material can be recycled and reused for six successive runs without deteriorating the catalytic activity. A powder XRD pattern for the recovered solid catalyst showed that the structural integrity of the catalyst was maintained during the catalytic process.
## 4.6 Urea
The heterogeneous catalysis of MOFs with urea as the functional group has been seldom explored in literature. Farha, Scheidt and Hupp et al. developed a urea-containing MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})_{2}(\mathrm{cada})$ (NU-601; bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine; $\mathbf{H_{4}c a d a}=5{,}5^{\prime}.$ - (carbonylbis(azanediyl))diisophthalic acid).78 MOF NU-601 possessed large channels in all directions ( $\dot{a}$ axis, $12.05\times13.95\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , $b$ axis, $11.38\times13.69\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ , and $c$ axis, $11.38\times4.87\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ ; atom-toatom separations) with $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ urea bonds projecting into the pores. The catalytic activity of activated NU-601 was examined for the Friedel–Crafts reaction between pyrroles and nitroalkenes (Fig. 45). The reaction between $N\cdot$ -methylpyrrole and $(E)$ -1-nitroprop1-ene with $10\mathrm{mol\%}$ NU-601 in 1 : $\mathrm{\midMeNO_{2}/T H F}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ finished with $98\%$ consumption of $(E)$ -1-nitroprop-1-ene after $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ . To confirm that the active catalyst species did not leach into solution, filtration experiments were performed. After the removal of NU-601, the reaction of the filtrate shut down rapidly, suggesting that the reaction was heterogeneous in nature. NU-601 was reusable, maintaining its reactivity after one cycle of use and experiencing only a slight degradation of reactivity after five cycles. The solid MOF catalyst retained its crystallinity as determined by powder XRD. To investigate whether the catalysis took place on the surface or within the pores of MOF NU-601, larger substrates were used as steric probes. Toward this end, when 1-phenethyl-1H-pyrrole and/or $(E)$ -(4-nitrobut-3-en-1-yl)-benzene were employed, the substrate consumption and reaction yield decreased rapidly. Such reagent size selectivity provided evidence that the catalytic reactions mostly occurred within the cavities of the MOF catalyst.
## 4.7 Binol
As a type of specific organic ligand that can generate intrinsically chiral MOFs, the binol-type ligands have been early developed by Lin’s group to synthesize homochiral MOFs for asymmetric catalysis. They designed two chiral ligands derived from $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -bi-2-naphthol (binol), namely $(R)\mathbf{-}6,6^{\prime}$ -dichloro- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ - dihydroxy- $^{\cdot1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (binol-1) and $(R)\cdot2,2^{\prime}\cdot$ dihydroxy- $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}}$ -tetrabenzoate (binol-2).79 Self-assembly of the two ligands and different salts resulted in the formation of MOFs of $[\mathbf{Cd}_{3}\mathbf{Cl}_{6}(\mathbf{binol}{-1})_{3}]$ $\mathrm{\cdot4DMF{\cdot}6M e O H{\cdot}3H_{2}O}$ $\operatorname{CdCl}_{2}$ -binol-1), $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{3}(b i n o l-1)_{4}(N O_{3})_{6}]}\cdot7M e O H\cdot5H_{2}O}$ $\displaystyle(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{-}$ binol-1), $\small\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b i n o l-2)(d m f)(H_{2}O)]}.$ 2EtOH4.3DMF $\mathrm{4H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Zn-binol-2) and $[(\mathrm{binol{-}2})\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{solvents})_{2}]$ (Cu-binol-2), which possess large channels with the size of $16\times18\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , $14\times14\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , $15\times20\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , and $30\times20\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , respectively. Treatment of these homochiral MOFs with excess $\mathrm{Ti-(OiPr)_{4}}$ produced Ti-containing MOFs in which the dihydroxy groups of binol were coordinated with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ salts (Fig. 46). These Ti-containing MOFs were found to be able to perform as active catalysts in the $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ addition reactions with aromatic aldehydes. In the case of 1-naphthaldehyde, the product $(R)$ -1-(1-naphthyl)propanol was obtained with high yields and excellent ee values in the presence of the Ti-containing MOFs derived from $\mathbf{CdCl}_{2}$ -binol-1 $(93\%)$ , $\operatorname{Cd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ -binol-1 $(90\%)$ and Cu-binol-2 $(91\%)$ . However, only $22\%$ ee was obtained in the reaction catalyzed by the post-synthesized catalyst from Zn-binol-2. In order to study the dependence of the enantiomeric excesses on the open channel sizes, they prepared later a series of isoreticular chiral MOFs of Cu-binol-2 based on tetracarboxylate bridging ligands with different length derived from $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -bi-2-naphthol (binol) and copper paddle-wheel secondary building units (SBUs).79d Catalytic results showed that the enantiomeric excesses of 1-phenyl-1-propanol that resulted from the addition of diethylzinc to benzaldehyde were indeed dependent on the CMOF channel sizes.
## 4.8 Bipyridyl
A representative example of heterogeneous catalysis utilizing the bipyridyl functional group in MOF materials was established on the basis of porous MOFs containing free uncoordinative bipyridyl organic sites. Yaghi et al. prepared MOF-253 (Al(OH)(bpydc); bpydc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) in which the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ - bipyridine (bpy) moieties are not coordinated with any metal ions.80a Considering that organic molecules that contain bidentate chelating centers, such as $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylethane-1,2-diamine, 1,10- phenanthroline, and quinoline-1-amino-2-carboxylic acid could catalyze the direct arylation of arenes, Li et al. then tested the catalytic activity of MOF-253 in similar reactions (Fig. 47).80b For initial optimization, the cross-coupling of 4-iodoanisole with benzene was selected as the model reaction. When three equivalents of $\mathbf{\nabla}\kappa\mathbf{o}^{t}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}$ were used in the presence of $30~\mathrm{mol\%}$ of MOF-253, the reaction furnished the product in $99\%$ yield after $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Surprisingly, the efficiency was dramatically decreased when the same amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}^{\cdot\dagger}$ bpydc was used to replace MOF-253. These results indicated that the catalytic efficiency of bpydc ligands in the cross-couplings could be improved significantly by assembling them within a MOF structure.
Later, Li et al. further used MOF-253 for the immobilization of CuI.80c The activated MOF-253 was soaked in an acetonitrile solution of CuI to give rise to MOF-253 0.5CuI ( ${\mathrm{8.3~wt\%}}$ Cu), which is determined by elemental analysis. As depicted in Fig. 47, thus functionalized MOF-253 0.5CuI was then employed in the C–O cross-coupling of phenols and alcohols with aryl halides. Under the optimized reaction conditions, the reactions between phenols and aryl halides could lead to the ethers with up to $97\%$ yield. The reusability of the catalyst system was also tested, and no significant efficiency loss was observed after three runs. The powder XRD experiments of the catalyst after the third run displayed no significant changes in the crystalline structure of the MOF after reactions, and the AAS experiments on the reaction mixture showed that a very low Cu leaching amount ( ${<0.1\%}$ of the total Cu) was detected in solution.
## 4.9 Catechol
A judicious strategy to incorporate catechol into the MOF skeleton as the functional organic group for heterogeneous catalysis has been proposed by Farha, Hupp and Nguyen et al.81 They used MIL-101(Cr) for the post-synthesis incorporation of catechols that can then be further metallated with ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ as illustrated in Fig. 48. They chose dopamine (dop) as the catechol-bearing grafting agent, and successful dopamine grafting was achieved by exposing the dehydrated MIL-101(Cr) to a suspension of free-base dopamine in anhydrous THF at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . To get a better handle on dopamine loading, they protected its two hydroxyl functionalities with tert-butyldimethyl silyl (TBDMS) groups prior to grafting, and then used ICP-OES to determine the molar ratio of Si to Cr. ICP analysis indicated a $\mathrm{si/cr}$ ratio of $\sim0.10$ (equivalent to only $5\%$ dopamine loading). These results pointed to modification being limited to $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites at or near the MOF external surface. Thus the obtained $\mathrm{MIL-101(Cr)\mathrm{-}(T B D M S)_{2}d o p}$ was then exposed to a considerably smaller amine 2-(methylthio)ethylamine as a secondary modifier, which might access the internal surfaces of the porous MOF. After deprotection, MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathrm{(TBDMS)_{2}d o p}$ converted to MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})\ –\mathrm{dop}$ , which can be readily metallated with ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ to give a material with a $\mathbf{V}/\mathbf{Cr}$ ratio of $\sim0.05$ , the maximum possible for a stoichiometry of one catechol per vanadium. They then evaluated the catalytic activity of MIL-101(Cr)-dop-V in oxidation of thioanisole. In the presence of TBHP, MIL-101(Cr)-dop-V was capable to catalyze the oxidation of thioanisole to the corresponding sulfoxide and sulfone with 57 and $26\%$ yield, respectively. Furthermore, the MIL-101(Cr)-dop-V catalyst can be recovered and re-used without any loss in total sulfide conversion.
In summary, functional organic sites in MOFs have developed from simple functional groups such as pyridyl, amine and amide in early work to relatively complex and more valuable FOS such as proline and derivatives, NHCs and urea, all of which are perfect organocatalysts. L-Proline is a natural substance, and thus homochiral MOFs derived from it or its derivatives can be well designed and synthesized. Although there is an extensive repertoire of chiral NHCs and ureas, they haven’t been incorporated into MOFs. As shown in the available reports, NHCs in MOFs are merely based on imidazolium salts. Other NHC sources such as triazolium, tetrazolium, pyrazolium, oxazolium and thiazolium salts have not been used for the synthesis of NHC-containing MOFs. On the other hand, FOS can be used for the immobilization of metal catalysts, of which amino groups are most studied. This might be ascribed to a variety of aminocontaining MOFs such as amino-substituted IRMOFs, UiOs and MILs available. As exemplified here, bipyridyl, NHCs, catechol, etc. can well bond with metal complexes. To explore the rich catalysis of MOFs, efforts might be driven to design more suitable ligands with these valuable functional groups for MOF synthesis.
## 5. MNPs@MOF
The inherent host–guest chemistry of porous MOF materials allows the implementation of desired properties by filling the framework cavities with various guest molecules and clusters. In particular, the doping of MOFs with metal nanoparticles (MNPs) is of interest for heterogeneous catalysis.82–95 It is expected that the crystalline porous structures of MOFs can limit the migration and aggregation of MNPs, considering that the typical synthesis of MNPs inside MOFs (MNPs@MOF) is through a stepwise process, that is, precursor infiltration and subsequent decomposition that converts the precursor into nanoparticles inside the framework cavity. Until now, a few catalytically active metals (e.g., Cu, Ru, Au, Pd, Pt) have been finely dispersed into the MOF cavities (e.g. MOF-5, MOF-177, MIL-101(Cr), ZIF-8, ZIF-90), among which Au@MOF and $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{MOF}$ have been largely studied compared to others.
The widely used approach to prepare MNPs@MOFs (or sometimes denoted as MNPs/MOFs) is to use MOFs as stabilizing host materials providing a confined space for nucleation, which includes techniques such as chemical vapor deposition, solution infiltration, and solid grinding for introducing the metal precursor (e.g. $\mathbf{ML}_{n_{\star}}^{\star}$ . The intermediates, namely precursor loaded materials $[\mathbf{ML}_{n}]_{m}@\mathbf{MOF}$ , are subsequently converted to $\mathbf{MNPS}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}N O F s}$ in hydrogen or with other reducing agents such as $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . It is mandatory to choose precursors with decomposition conditions that will be tolerated by the host MOF framework. Therefore, the thermal stability of the MOF framework should match the corresponding decomposition temperature of the precursor in order to obtain nanoparticles in an unchanged host matrix. In the case of impregnation of MOFs in solution, metal complexes or salts are diluted in adequate solvents and subsequently used for impregnation. After removal of the solvents, the metal precursors can decompose to the metal by further treatment.
The concept of solvent-free gas-phase infiltration to yield precursor loaded MOF materials has been introduced by Fischer in 2005.82b In comparison to the impregnation of MOFs in solution and similar to the solid-grinding method, this gas-phase infiltration benefits from the absence of competition between solvent and metal precursor molecules, and therefore allows a much wider range of metal loadings, even above $40~\mathrm{wt\%}$ metal in a single step. On the other hand, the solution based synthesis strategy for the deposition of metals inside MOFs can avoid the use of highly reactive and air sensitive precursors, which are usually applied in the solvent-free gasphase infiltration method. In Table 5 are summarized the selective literature studies relevant to the loading of metal nanoparticles on MOFs. Early studies focused on the use of the well-known MOF-5 as the scaffold, while more recent work extended to MIL-101(Cr). The excellent chemical and thermal stability of the latter MOF has facilitated the fair evaluation of the new properties of the composites. MNPs@MOFs have been employed in heterogeneous catalysis such as methanol synthesis, hydrogenation of hydrocarbons, reduction of ketones, oxidation of alcohols, CO oxidation, C–C bond formation and indole formation reaction. Especially, $\mathbf{Au@MOF}$ catalyzed alcohol oxidation and Pd@MOF (usually $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{MIL}\mathbf{-}101(\mathbf{Cr}))$ 1 catalyzed C–C bond formation reactions have attracted a great deal of attention.
## 5.1 Cu@MOF and Ru@MOFs
MOF-5 (IRMOF-1, $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3},$ , which was developed by Yaghi et al., is a suitable host for various types of loading studies due to its facile synthesis even on a larger scale, temperature stability up to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in argon, the relatively large pore opening of $7.8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ and a high Langmuir surface area of up to $4500~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .82a Depending on the tilting of the bdc linkers, MOF-5 can be considered to exhibit two kinds of pore diameters with 11.0 and $15.1\mathring\mathrm{A}.$
By way of metal organic chemical vapor deposition (MOCVD), Fischer’s group successfully loaded the porous framework of MOF-5 with volatile complexes such as $\mathrm{cpCu}(\mathbf{PMe}_{3})$ , $\mathrm{CpCu}(\mathrm{CN}^{t}\mathrm{Bu})$ and $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{cod}})({\mathrm{cot}})$ , leading to the formation of $[\mathrm{CpCu}(\mathrm{PMe}_{3})]_{2}(\mathfrak{A})$ MOF-5, $[\mathrm{CpCu}(\mathrm{CN}^{t}\mathrm{Bu})]@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ , and $[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{cod})(\mathrm{cot})]_{3.5}\textcircled{\div}\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ , respectively.82,83 The intermediates $[\mathrm{CpCu}(\mathrm{PMe}_{3})]_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ and $[\mathrm{CpCu}(\mathrm{CN}^{t}\mathrm{Bu})]@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ have been found to be able to convert into the respective materials $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ by a variety of methods, such as photoassisted thermolysis at $90-100~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ under inert gas, purely thermal hydrogenolysis at $200{-}220\ {^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ or photoassisted hydrogenolysis.82c,83 A loading of about $10–11\mathrm{wt\%}$ Cu is obtained in the final material $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ within one loading cycle. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption studies revealed type I isotherms and gave values of $1000{-}1100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the equivalent Langmuir surface, providing evidence that the resulting materials $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ are still highly porous. Comparison of the powder XRD patterns of desolvated MOF-5 with $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ verified that the characteristic reflection pattern of the crystalline MOF-5 host material is nicely retained.
Transmission electron micrographs of the obtained $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ materials proved the presence of Cu particles of $1{-}3\ \mathrm{nm}$ that were homogeneously distributed over the MOF matrix. No particles or agglomerates outside the MOF matrix were observed. The measured X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) were compared with reference samples of bulk Cu, CuO, and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , revealing that $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ contains mainly $\mathrm{\Cu}(0)$ . The $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ samples have been tested as heterogeneous catalysts for methanol synthesis (Fig. 49), and the catalytic results afforded stable values of $70~\mathrm{\upmumol~MeOH~g_{cat}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{Cu@MOF{-}5}$ .
Furthermore, hydrogenolysis of $[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{cod})(\mathrm{cot})]_{3.5}\textcircled{\div}\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ yielded $\mathbf{Ru}\ @\mathbf{MOF}{\cdot}5$ with ruthenium nanoparticles $(30~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of Ru) in a typical size range of $1.5{-}1.7~\mathrm{nm}$ , embedded in the intact MOF-5 matrix, which has been confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and powder XRD measurements.83 From the type I isotherms of the ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption measurements of the composite, an equivalent Langmuir surface area of $860\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ was calculated. The resulting $\mathbf{Ru}\textcircled{\mathbf{2}}\mathbf{MOF}{\cdot}\mathbf{5}$ was tested as a catalyst in the hydrogenation of benzene, and the reaction was performed under 3 bar $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (Fig. 49). It was found that the reaction was complete in $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the conversion of benzene to cyclohexane was detected to be approximately $25\%$ .
## 5.2 Au@MOFs
Haruta’s group developed an effective method for the direct deposition of Au clusters onto MOFs, such as CPL-1 $\left([\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{pzdc})_{2}(\mathbf{pyz})]_{n}\right.$ , pzdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ pyrazine-2,3-dicarboxylate, pyz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ pyrazine), CPL-2 $\left([\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathsf{p z d c})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]_{n}\right)$ , MIL-53(Al), MOF-5, and HKUST-1, by a solid grinding process with a volatile organogold complex, $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{Au}(\mathbf{acac})$ .84 The $\mathbf{\mathbf{Au}}/\mathbf{PCPs}$ were prepared via the following procedure: $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{Au}$ (acac) (acac $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ acetylacetonate) and PCPs were ground in an agate mortar in air for $20~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, and then the mixture was treated in a stream of $10~\mathrm{vol\%}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , thus obtaining Au/PCPs by simply solid grinding. From TEM observations, Au clusters were found to be larger than the pore size and were mostly placed on the outer surfaces of PCPs. Au/PCPs exhibited moderate to high catalytic activity for the aerobic oxidation of alcohols in the liquid phase. In the presence of the base of ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ , benzyl alcohol oxidation in methanol with Au/CPL-1,
Au/CPL-2 and Au/HKUST-1 yielded benzaldehyde as the major product, whereas the same reaction with Au/MIL-53(Al) and $\mathbf{Au/MOF{-}}5$ gave methyl benzoate as the major product (Fig. 50). Since the catalytic Au clusters are actually attached on the surface of MOF materials, these catalytic reactions may not make use of their porous properties.
Alternatively, ZIF-8 is desirable for depositing metal nanoparticles into the framework pores because it holds an intersecting 3D structural feature, high thermal stability (over $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , large pore size (diameter of 11.6 Å), and large surface area (BET, $1413\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ .47a
By a simple solid grinding method, Xu groups supported Au NPs with ZIF-8.85 The Au@ZIF-8 was prepared by grinding the pretreated ZIF-8 and desired quantitative volatile organogold complex (to give 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, and $5.0~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Au loadings, respectively) $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{Au}$ (acac) uniformly in an agate mortar in air for $\sim35$ min at room temperature. Then the as-prepared sample was treated in a stream of $10~\mathrm{vol\%}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in He at $230^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2.5\mathrm{{h}}$ . The A $\operatorname{u}(\widehat{\mathbf{a}})Z\operatorname{IF}^{-8}$ samples were catalytically active in CO oxidation (Fig. 51). TEM images showed that the mean diameters are $3.4\pm1.4$ and $3.1\pm0.9\mathrm{{nm}}$ for $1.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ Au@ZIF-8 before and after reaction, respectively, whereas the average diameters are $4.2\pm2.6$ and $3.5\pm2.4~\mathrm{nm}$ or $5.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}{-}8$ before and after catalysis, respectively. The crystalline order of the ZIF-8 host matrix mostly remains unchanged after loading Au NPs and several runs of reactions, which has been confirmed by the comparison of the powder XRD patterns. There were no obvious diffractions characteristic of Au NPs observed from the patterns of $\mathrm{\DeltaAu}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ after catalysis, which agrees with TEM observation.
By means of solvent free gas-phase infiltration (MOCVD) method, Fischer group successfully synthesized Au@ZIF-8 and $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-90}$ , which were prepared by the sublimation of the volatile gold complex $\mathtt{A u(C O)C l}$ into the activated MOFs in static vacuum (0.5 bar) at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ and the subsequent hydrogenation of the obtained $\mathtt{\left[A u(C O)C l\right]}\ @\mathbf{MOF}$ materials under hydrogen (2 bar) with temperatures varying from $100~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ $\left(\mathsf{A u@Z I F^{-8}}\right)$ to $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\left(\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-90}\right)$ .86 The loading was varied between 5 and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (i.e., up to $33\mathrm{\mol\%}$ ). The powder XRD data reveal intact MOF frameworks after the incorporation of gold. Neither the peak positions nor the line shapes were altered significantly. The resulting BET surface areas obtained by standard nitrogen adsorption measurements are between 1334 and $787~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ (10 and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Au) and 1004 and $632~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Au@ZIF-90 (10 and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Au). Highresolution TEM imaging indicated that a broad size distribution of $_{1-5\mathrm{nm}}$ and a narrower size regime of 1–2 were obtained for $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}{-}8$ and $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-90}$ , respectively. The catalytic activities of Au@ZIFs were tested in the oxidation of alcohols, and the results showed that $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}{-}8$ had a very high activity whereas $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF-90}$ was inactive (Fig. 51). Similar to $\mathbf{Au/MIL}$ 53(Al) and Au/MOF-5 prepared by solid grinding method,84 $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ catalyzed oxidation reaction of benzyl alcohol to produce methyl benzoate as the major product.
Compared to other MOFs, MIL-101(Cr) represents probably the most widely used catalyst support for deposition of MNPs due to its high chemical and thermal stability, large surface area, and huge cavities. By employing a simple colloidal method with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as protecting agent, Li and coworkers developed a highly efficient heterogeneous gold catalyst of $\mathbf{Au/MIL-101(Cr)}.$ 87 After the mixture of an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ and PVP was vigorously stirred in an ice bath for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ , an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ was added to the mixture, giving rise to the formation of the Au:PVP colloids, which were immediately deposited onto the MIL-101(Cr) support. The Au/MIL-101(Cr) was obtained by the hydrogenation of the performed Au:PVP composites at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the loading of Au and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ reduction, there was no apparent loss of crystallinity as testified by the powder XRD patterns, indicating that the framework of MIL-101(Cr) was mostly retained. The gold content was estimated to be about $0.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , measured by AAS analysis. The HRTEM image of the obtained Au/MIL-101(Cr) demonstrated that small gold particles $\left(<3~\mathrm{\nm}\right)$ could be encapsulated inside the pores of MIL-101(Cr). Au/MIL-101(Cr) displayed extremely high catalytic activities in liquid-phase aerobic oxidation of a wide range of alcohols (Fig. 52).
## 5.3 Pd@MOFs
By an incipient wetness infiltration of the $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{acac})_{2}$ ( $\tt a c a c=$ acetylacetonate) precursor followed by reduction under vacuum or using hydrogen flow at different temperatures, Kaskel’s group prepared Pd nanoparticles loaded MOF-5.88a With this incipient wetness technique, the precursor was dissolved in solvent (e.g. $\mathbf{CHCl}_{3.}^{\cdot}$ ), and the volume of the solvent applied in the loading procedure was estimated from the free volume of the porous material used. The metal loading was determined to be $1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ by elemental analysis. The BET surface area of the composite material was found to be reduced in comparison to the starting material, from 2885 to $958~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The catalytic activity of Pd/MOF-5 has been tested for styrene, 1-octene and cis-cyclooctene in a static hydrogen atmosphere. The catalytic evaluation showed that the palladium loaded MOF-5 had a high catalytic activity in styrene hydrogenation comparable to that of palladium on activated carbon, but cis-cyclooctene hydrogenation was considerably slower. However, the as-prepared Pd/MOF-5 was unstable in contact with water or humid air, which is due to the low hydrothermal stability of the MOF-5 support.
Pd/MOF-5 has also been prepared by Seayad’s group through a modified procedure.88 $^c\mathrm{K_{2}P d C l_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ were employed as a precursor and a reducing agent, respectively. The Pd contents vary from 0.25 to $3\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The HR-TEM images showed that the sizes of the Pd nanoparticles were in the range of 3 to $12\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The Pd NPs were well dispersed on the surface of MOF-5. The obtained Pd/MOF-5 was used as a catalyst in the aminocarbonylation reaction. Results indicated that Pd/MOF was a renewable heterogeneous catalyst for aminocarbonylation among aryl iodides, amines and CO, with a wide substrate scope related to both of aryl iodides and amines.
To develop an air-stable Pd/MOF catalyst, Kaskel’s group further tested to support palladium in MIL-101(Cr) via the similar incipient wetness impregnation method.88b The dispersion of palladium in the fresh Pd/MIL-101 material was analysed via hydrogen chemisorption, suggesting that the diameter of the palladium particles was around $1.5\mathrm{nm}$ . Compared to $\mathbf{Pd/MOF{-}}5$ , Pd/MIL-101(Cr) $(1\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd) manifested a better catalytic performance in the hydrogenation of olefins.
Alternatively, Li’s group prepared Pd nanoparticles supported in the MOF by impregnation of activated MIL-101 with a ${\bf P d(N O_{3})}_{2}$ precursor diluted in DMF at room temperature, followed by placing under a stream of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 hours to yield Pd/MIL-101(Cr).89 The powder XRD measurements confirmed that the palladium loading $\left(c a.\ 1\ \mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ did not result in any apparent loss of MOF crystallinity, indicating that the framework integrity of the MIL-101(Cr) was well maintained. The TEM image showed that the palladium NPs were highly dispersed, with a mean diameter of $(1.9\pm0.7)\$ nm. The XPS investigation of Pd/MIL-101(Cr) at the palladium 3d level indicated that most of the palladium was in the reduced form. The $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ catalyst was found to be effective in Suzuki– Miyaura and Ullmann coupling reactions of aryl chlorides as well as the one-step synthesis of methyl isobutyl ketone (Fig. 53). However, the unreduced $\mathbf{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}/\mathbf{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was ineffective in the reactions, suggesting that palladium in the reduced form should act as the active site.
Cao’s group demonstrated that Pd/MIL-101(Cr), which was prepared through solution infiltration of activated MIL-101(Cr)
with $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ as a precursor and subsequent hydrogenation with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , exhibited extremely high catalytic activities in the direct C2 arylation of substituted indoles with iodobenzenes at $120~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (Fig. 53).90a The powder XRD patterns showed that the frameworks of MIL-101(Cr) did not alter after the Pd loading ( $(c a.\ 0.5\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ . TEM images of Pd/MIL-101(Cr) indicated that most of the dispersed Pd NPs were successfully encapsulated in the cages of the MIL-101(Cr), with a mean diameter of $\left(2.6\pm0.5\right)\mathrm{nm}$ . The XPS and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) data revealed that $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ metal sites coexisted in the $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ doped materials. In the XPS traces, the $3\mathsf{d}^{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}^{3/2}$ peaks of $\mathrm{{\bfPd}}^{0}$ were observed at 336.2 and $341.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and no obvious peaks corresponding to $\mathbf{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions were detected. More recently, they modified the procedure of the Pd/MIL-101(Cr)-catalyzed C2 arylation, employing arylbornic acids instead of iodobenzenes.90c With the modified procedure, the indole formation reaction can take place under milder reaction conditions, and doesn’t require high temperature.
The same group also used $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathbf{Cr})$ as the catalyst support, and successfully encapsulated Pd nanoparticles by ionic reaction of ammonium groups with $\mathrm{[PdCl_{4}]^{2-}}$ and a subsequent reduction with $\mathrm{{NaBH}_{4}}$ .90b Based on inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) data, they estimated that a $0.62~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of palladium was loaded in the porous $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{-MIL-}101(\mathbf{Cr})$ framework. The bright-field and dark-field TEM images suggested that most of the dispersed Pd NPs were included in the cages of the $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Cr})$ MOF framework, with a mean diameter of $2.49\mathrm{nm}$ . The XPS and EDS data revealed that most of the palladium atoms were in the reduced form. The doped Pd $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Cr) MOF was effective in the dehalogenation of aryl chlorides in water with $\mathrm{\bfHCOONH_{4}}$ . Using a similar ion-exchanged method, Cao’s group also prepared Pd nanoparticles supported on amine-functionalized, mixed-linker MOFs (MIXMOFs) based on MIL-53(Al).90d The mean diameter of Pd NPs was about $3.2~\mathrm{nm}$ . The Pd/MIXMOF displayed high catalytic activity for Heck reaction and can be easily recovered and reused.
On the other hand, Li’s group embedded Pd nanoparticles within MIL-101(Cr) via an incipient wetting procedure with $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{acac})_{2}$ $(0.5{-}4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd) as the precursor, followed by the reduction in a $10\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{Ar}$ flow at $493\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .91 The almost unchanged XRD patterns of $3\%{\bf-P d/M I L-}101$ suggested that the Pd incorporation occurred with no apparent loss of MOF structural integrity. Through the measurement of CO chemisorption, the Pd nanoparticle size was calculated to be around $2.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-101(Cr)}$ (3 wt% Pd). This conclusion was further verified by STEM images of $3\%$ -doped Pd/MIL-101(Cr) MOF, which suggested that the mean diameter of Pd nanoparticles was $2.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The catalytic properties of the resulting $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ samples were evaluated in an atom-economy synthesis of indole in water. In the presence of the Pd/MIL101(Cr) catalyst $\left(1.0\mathrm{mol\%\Pd}\right)$ , 2-iodoaniline as well as a variety of substituted 2-iodoaniline could react with phenylacetylene to produce the corresponding indoles with high yields (up to $98\%$ ) (Fig. 53).
In contrast to the studies mentioned above, Kempe’s group reported the synthesis of $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{MIL}\mathbf{-}101\big(\mathbf{Cr}\big)$ by means of the metal–organic chemical vapor deposition method. The volatile complex $\left(\eta^{5}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{5}\mathbf{H}_{5}\right)\mathbf{Pd}\left(\eta^{3}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{5}\right)$ was chosen as the precursor, and the loading of the complex in the MIL-101(Cr) framework was identified to be higher than $50~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ .92 Quantitative reduction of the Pd precursor complex with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ gave rise to Pd NPs inside the MIL-101(Cr) pores $\mathbf{\widetilde{(Pd@MIL^{-101}(C r))}}$ . The generation of $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}101\big(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\big)$ at low temperatures $(21^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ and low $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure (5 bar) led to the Pd nanoparticles with around $2.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ size, whereas the nanoparticles with smaller size (about $1.7\ \mathrm{\nm}$ ) were formed within MIL-101 when adjusting the conditions to elevated temperature $(70^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ and higher pressure (70 bar). The synthesized $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{MIL-}101$ samples were investigated as heterogeneous catalysts in the solvent-free reduction of ketones (Fig. 53). Both of $\mathbf{Pd\widehat{\sf{a}}M I L\cdot101}$ materials prepared at high and low temperatures can effectively promote the reduction of aromatic ketones. In general, $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{MIL-}101$ showed higher conversions for smaller substrates while lower conversions for bigger substrates.
## 5.4 Pt@MOFs
Similar to the Pd atom with wide catalytic potential in organic reactions, the Pt atom is also of interest to chemists and has been applied in MOF catalysis through integration as nanoparticles. The larger pore opening and pore volume of MOF-177 $(10.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $1.59\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~}$ , in comparison to MOF-5 (7.8 Å and $1.04\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ), allows the diffusion of larger molecules into the MOF cavities, as well as the overall absorption of more molecules per cavity than in MOF-5.93a By means of the MOCVD method, Kempe’s group successfully accomplished the gasphase loading of MOF-177 with the volatile platinum precursor $\bf{M e}_{3}P t C p^{\prime}$ $\mathrm{Cp^{\prime}=}$ methylcyclopentadienyl).93b Subsequent reduction of the inclusion compound $[\mathbf{Me}_{3}\mathbf{PtCp^{\prime}}]_{4}@\mathbf{MOF}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{1}77$ by hydrogen at 100 bar and $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ gave rise to the formation of platinum nanoparticles in MOF-177 host lattice $\mathbf{\left(Pt@MOF^{-177}\right.}$ ). TEM images of the Pt-loaded $\mathrm{Pt@MOF^{-177}}$ hybrid materials showed that the size of the platinum nanoparticles was mainly between 2 and $3\mathrm{nm}$ . ICP-MS measurements indicated that $43\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of platinum was loaded in the MOF-177 framework. Such Pt@MOF-177 has been tested in the oxidation reaction of alcohols. Under the optimized reaction conditions, allylic alcohols were oxidized smoothly with high selectivity.
More recently, Xu’s group used the ‘‘double solvents’’ method to introduce ultrafine Pt nanoparticles into MIL-101(Cr) pores without Pt nanoparticles aggregation on the external surface of the framework.94 The ‘‘double solvents’’ included a hydrophilic solvent (water) and a hydrophobic solvent (hexane), the former containing the metal precursor $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\right)$ whereas the latter playing an important role in suspending the adsorbent and facilitating the impregnation process. There was no loss of crystallinity in the powder XRD patterns after $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ reduction, suggesting that the integrity of the MIL-101(Cr) framework was maintained after the host Pt@MIL-101(Cr) loading Pt up to $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The TEM and electron tomographic measurements clearly demonstrated the uniform 3D distribution of the ultrafine Pt nanoparticles was achieved with the average size of $1.8\pm0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ throughout the interior cavities of MIL-101(Cr) pores. The synthesized Pt@MIL-101(Cr) displayed excellent catalytic activities for reactions in all three phases, including liquid-phase ammonia borane hydrolysis, solid-phase ammonia borane thermal dehydrogenation and gas-phase CO oxidation reactions (Fig. 54).
Another example of $\mathrm{\bfPt}$ -doped MOF catalysis comes from Gascon’s group, who developed the synthetic strategy by encapsulation of the Pt-supported phosphotungstic acid (PTA) in the $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathbf{A}$ l) framework.95 The grafting of platinum was performed by mixing an ethanolic solution of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ with a suspension of PTA- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}.$ -MIL-101(Al) in ethanol for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . Indicated by the XPS and ICP-OES data of $\mathrm{Pt/PTA–NH_{2}}$ -MIL-101(Al), the atomic ratios of $\mathrm{Pt/Al}$ , W/Al and $\mathrm{Pt/W}$ were estimated to be 0.33, 2.02 and 0.165, respectively. After reduction with hydrogen, the resulting hybrid catalysts have been tested in the oxidation of CO, the preferential oxidation of CO in the presence of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and the hydrogenation of toluene. The catalytic results showed that the Pt/PTA-MOF catalyst was more active than a commercial $\mathbf{Pt/Al_{2}O_{3}}$ reference catalyst.
In general, different metal nanoparticles can be formed in the cavities or the surfaces of MOFs, and exhibited rich catalytic behaviors. Different preparation methods, including gas phase infiltration, impregnation, solid grinding and colloidal, have profound influences on the metal loading as well as the size/shape of the nanoparticles. As far as catalytic results are concerned, MOFs, as the hosts, play an important role in the selectivity of the products. As exemplified in Au/MOFs catalyzed benzyl alcohol oxidation, Au/CPL-1, Au/CPL-2 and Au/HKUST-1 provided benzaldehyde as the major product, whereas Au/MIL53(Al), Au/MOF-5 and Au@ZIF-8 yielded methyl benzoate as the major one.
## 6. MOFs for photo- and biomimetic catalysis
In more specific sense, supramolecular catalysis by way of porous MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts has been elegantly demonstrated by recent state-of-art progress in MOF-based biomimetic catalysis and photocatalysis. $^{14e,17c,22}$ To learn ideas from nature to incorporate the biomimetic effect and photochemical transformation into MOF catalysts provide novel perspectives on tackling some long-lasting problems, such as inefficient enzyme-mimic catalysis, and ever-increasing global energy and environmental crisis. In these aspects, excellent reviews have been presented by Zhou22a and $\mathrm{Wu}^{22b}$ to introduce MOFs as heterogeneous biomimetic catalysts in the applications of peroxidase, cytochromes $\mathbf{P}450$ and hemoglobin biomimicry, and by $\mathrm{Lin}^{17c}$ and Garcia14e to introduce MOF-catalyzed and photodriven proton reduction, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and organic transformations. Herein, we only discuss a few examples to highlight their catalytic applications in an aqueous environment.
Implantation of biomimetic active sites, such as metalloporphyrin motifs, into thermal and chemical stable MOF frameworks is able to stabilize the catalytic centers, create reactive pockets and diffusion-favored hierarchical structures, thus facilitating substrate recognition, enhancing catalytic efficiency, and increasing shape- and size-selectivity superior to the homogeneous counterparts. In nature, metalloporphyrins are well known for performing many biological functions in aqueous media, such as light harvesting, oxygen transportation, and forming cofactors for many enzyme/protein families. We have introduced relevant research works on porous MOFs containing metalloporphyrinic frameworks55–58 as biomimetic catalysts in Section 3.1.
In general, the porous MOFs integrating metalloporphyrinic frameworks as SBUs are not always suitable for biomimetic catalysis, in case they are constructed from carboxylate ligands and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ and ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ cations.55–57 This is because these ions show higher affinities for water molecules than the carboxylate ligands, and thus the corresponding frameworks are inevitably unstable in aqueous media. However, the high valent metal ions, such as $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Sc^{4+}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , are more reactive to carboxylate ligands, affording porous MOFs with satisfactory thermal, chemical and moist stability. Excellent examples include MIL and UiO series MOFs.
Encouraged by this finding, Zhou et al. endeavored to synthesize $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -porphyrinic MOFs with the attempt to obtain catalytic MOFs under aqueous conditions.58 For example, they prepared PCN-222(Fe) which is related to cytochromes P450 in structural nature.58a The prosthetic group of P450 consists of an iron protoporphyrin IX, which is protected by polypeptides. These enzymes are potent oxidants capable of catalyzing many useful oxidation reactions. All cytochrome P450 enzymes are involved in a complex multienzymatic system, which hampers their industrial applications. The newly-developed PCN-222(Fe) is an ideal synthetic platform for mimicking cytochrome P450 enzymes owing to its stability, mesoporosity and high density of metalloporphyrin centers, and exhibited excellent peroxidase-like catalytic activity (Fig. 55). For the pyrogallol oxidation reaction, the $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ of PCN-222(Fe) was seven times higher than that of free hemin, indicating that PCN222(Fe) was a much more powerful catalyst than free hemin. Moreover, the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value of PCN-222(Fe) was lower than that of natural horseradish peroxidase enzyme, suggesting that PCN222(Fe) had a better affinity of the substrate. The excellent catalytic performance of PCN-222(Fe) was due to the large open channels $\left(3.7\mathrm{nm}\right)$ , which effectively prevented the selfdimerization of porphyrin centers and made it easy for substrates to enter the pores and bind to the catalytic centers.
On the other hand, natural photosynthesis is well-known to be utilized by green plants to achieve various photochemical transformations, such as water splitting and carbon dioxide reduction. These light-driven photochemical reactions can convert sunlight energy to chemical energy. To make use of MOFs for light harvesting and photocatalysis may take the advantages to easily integrate multiple functional components into the hierarchically organized MOF framework to accomplish highly efficient photocatalysts. Moreover, tailorable MOF frameworks readily allow incorporation of varied chromophores to utilize the full solar spectrum, especially visible region, with high efficiency. Three current strategies to develop MOF photocatalysts have been outlined and introduced in detail by $\mathrm{Lin}^{17c}$ and Garcia,14e which include: (1) employing MOF as a host for the encapsulation of a chromophore, (2) choosing photoresponsive organic ligands/metalloligands, and (3) exploring the semiconducting quantum dots properties of the metal nodes or encapsulated nanoparticles. It is proposed that the future trend for MOF photocatalysts is to develop MOFs possessing resistibility against water, since the modest hydrolytic and thermal stabilities of most MOFs prevent them from practical applications in solar energy utilization. We herein introduce two recent reports about stable MOF photocatalysts and their catalytic applications in aqueous solution.96
Ott and Cohen have successfully incorporated an effective proton reduction catalyst $[\mathrm{FeFe](bdt)(CO)_{6}}$ (bdt $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzenedithiolate) into MOFs (Fig. 56).96a 2,3-Dimercaptoterephthalic acid was prepared firstly and then complexed with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{CO})_{12}$ , yielding the complex $\mathrm{[FeFe](dcbdt)(CO)_{6}}$ (dcbdt $^{=1,4}$ -dicarboxybenzene-2,3-ditiolate). This metalloligand reacted with $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{{\left(rv\right)}}$ salts directly and failed to produce crystalline materials, resulting in the decomposition of the cluster. Instead, UiO-66-[FeFe]dcbdt $\mathrm{(CO)}_{6}$ was obtained through the ligand exchange of $\mathrm{[FeFe](dcbdt)(CO)_{6}}$ with bdc in UiO-66. A series of physical characterization techniques such as EDX, EA, TGA and $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR showed that $14\%$ of bdc was exchanged for $\mathrm{[FeFe](dcbdt)(CO)_{6}}$ . In conjunction with $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}3]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer and ascorbate as an electron donor, UiO-66- [FeFe]dcbdt $(\mathrm{CO})_{6}$ catalyzed photochemical hydrogen evolution in water at $\mathrm{pH}5$ , and exhibited higher performances than that of the free complex [FeFe]dcbd $\scriptstyle\{\mathbf{CO}\}_{6}$ in terms of rate and total hydrogen production yield. The superior catalytic performance of MOF might be due to the stabilization of the catalyst located within the framework.
$\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is a promising visible light photocatalyst due to its small band gap. However, its photocatalytic performance is limited by the high recombination rate of the photogenerated charge carriers. This can be overcome by reducing the particle dimensions. MOFs containing $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ -oxide clusters linked together in three dimensions by organic linkers can be promising photocatalysts. Roeffaers et al. examined the catalytic activities of several Fe-based MOFs such as MIL-100(Fe), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe), MIL-88B(Fe) and $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–88\mathbf{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ in the degradation of Rhodamine 6G in aqueous solution under visible light illumination $\left(350\mathrm{-}850\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ .96b Results showed that all of these $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ oxide based MOFs displayed remarkable visible light photocatalytic activity, and MIL-88B(Fe) exhibited the highest efficiency. In comparison, homogeneous iron(III) acetate clusters were also employed in the same reaction, however, no photocatalytic activity was measured under similar conditions. This might be due to the low stability of such clusters in water, whereas incorporating them into the MOF resulted in higher cluster stability.
## 7. Conclusion and perspective
This review makes an endeavor to outline the main advances and developments of MOFs for applications in heterogeneous supramolecular catalysis. After a brief introduction of unique porous attributes of MOFs on the background of MOMs selfassembly and crystal engineering, as well as their catalytic relevance and studying techniques, we started with catalytic applications of the well-known MILs series, such as MIL-101, MIL-100, MIL-53 and MIL-47 that have shown unusual thermal and chemical stability, and other purposely designed MOFs with CUMs with varied synthetic methodologies and catalytic activities. Afterwards, the functionalized MOFs for the purpose of heterogeneous catalysis have been classified and selectively introduced. The practical routes for functionalization of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts include direct synthesis, incorporating linkers with homogeneous catalysts, post-synthetic functionalization like either grafting of active groups on the open metal sites of certain structures or binding metal complexes to the free functional groups within frameworks, and encapsulation of metal nanoparticles inside the MOF pores. Finally, applications of MOFs in photo- and biomimetic catalysis are briefly introduced. Among various synthetic strategies proposed for MOF catalysis, the approach to loading metal nanoparticles, as well as active POMs and classic metal complex catalysts, into the porous matrices of MOFs may represent a newly developed and promising field of research in MOF catalysis that complements other extensively explored catalytic studies in which the catalytic activities are mainly based on the pure MOFs.
On the basis of abundant examples reported to use MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts under various conditions, it can be concluded that the crystalline and porous MOFs are good candidates as active and selective catalysts, or as catalyst host frameworks, for a wide variety of chemical reactions, varying from acid–base, redox to transition metal catalysis including $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{X}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{X}}\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}$ halide) formation. Especially, homochiral MOF catalyzed reactions can give rise to enantioselective compounds, which might have important potential in drug design and fine chemical synthesis. In addition, many of these studies demonstrated that the reasonable stability, heterogeneity and selectivity could be achieved by the coordination frameworks of MOFs. For example, MOF catalysis has been proved to be applicable even in the gas phase reactions like CO oxidation, which may help to get rid of the concerns about practical catalytic applications of MOF materials.
In this context, MOF materials should have a bright future in the catalytic synthesis of some important and valuable organic molecules because, for example, (1) they can be pre-designed on a rational basis according to a specific requirement of catalytic reaction, or deliberately tailored and modified to suit for a specific catalytic purpose, (2) the well-defined crystal structure of MOFs on the atomic level allows the explicit assessment of structure–activity relationships and application of the catalytic mechanism to a demanding reaction, (3) the uniform catalytic sites arrangement and their isolation in threedimensionality make them readily accessible by the substrates, which offers great advantages in light of the catalytic efficiency and economy in contrast to the bulky heterogeneous catalysts of which only the active sites on the solid surface are useful in catalytic reactions, and most important, (4) the intrinsic porous nature of the MOFs imposes a special confinement of the coordination space with the catalytic reactions taking place in a chemically and sterically tunable and modular microenvironment, which is of significance in a supramolecular sense for artificial synthesis mimicking natural enzymes that is difficult to accomplish by the small molecular catalysts.
Over the past ten years, more and more excellent studies on MOF catalysis have appeared. Such fast advances in the field of MOF catalysis may benefit from: (1) several well-established MOF materials are commercially available, such as Fe(BTC) (Basolite F 300), HKUST-1 (Basolite C 300) and MIL-53(Al) (Basolite A 100). This liberates the MOF materials from synthetic chemists, and largely expands and speeds up their applications in the catalytic study, (2) more and more thermally and chemically stable MOFs have been prepared, for example, Zr-containing MOFs which have been found to be highly stable up to $540~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and proven to be adequately stable against acid and moisture conditions, which have encouraged researchers to investigate more chemical reactions in a wider scope, (3) MOF catalysis starts to be of interest to organic chemists who will help to advance this burgeoning field to mature in a more rational and critical sense, and (4) rapid interdisciplinary study has appeared, for example, MOF catalysis has extended into the fields of nanoscience, photochemistry, biochemistry and medicine. This may booster some new research topics and open the doors to the broad scientific community.
Moreover, it may be expectable that MOF catalysis will continuously expand into more catalytic fields, such as photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, and even biocatalysis, because the self-assembly process of MOFs endows this kind of porous solid material with versatile properties with regard to photochemistry, electrochemistry, supramolecular chemistry and biochemistry. As classified in the Introduction of this review, 2nd to 4th generation of MOFs can all be utilized in MOF catalysis; however, understanding of the fact that the MOF structures can undergo a variety of post-processing procedures may put the MOF materials on a platform that new catalytic concepts and strategies could be created. The 3rd generation of MOFs should be paid more attention in-depth in catalytic sense, since their ability to be responsive to various external stimuli may generate more extensive catalytic skills with regard to environmental heat, light, pressure, mechanics, as well as chemical, physical, photo and magnetic induces. If the initial research interest in MOF catalysis is considered to start from the pure MOF materials containing active sites, the stage nowadays appreciate their ability to act as catalyst supports. The next step of MOF catalysis can move further to utilize MOF materials as catalyst precursors and base materials, for example, to grow MOF crystals in nanosize to form colloids, gels, and other hybrid materials that are suitable for catalytic study.5d Using MOF structures as precursors to generate metal oxide nanoparticles, metal-containing carbon materials, as well as hybrid polymeric and mesoporous solid materials, has been explored recently. These investigations may afford more chances for applications of MOF catalysis under extended conditions.
On the other hand, it is unlikely that MOF materials will replace other porous materials such as microporous zeolites and mesoporous silica, considering that MOFs are inferior to them in thermal and chemical stability. In addition, just as
MOFs can behave as hosts for metal complexes, metal ions substituting silicon atoms in the frameworks of zeolite and mesoporous silica can act as acid or redox active sites and may be used for different classes of catalytic reactions. Comparison among ZSM-5, MCM-41 and MIL-101(Cr), which represent the typical zeolite, mesoporous silica and MOF materials, respectively, is shown in Table 6. Upon evaluation of these three porous solid materials from different aspects (e.g. structure feature, surface area, stability and catalysis features), it can be concluded that these three porous solids possess rich catalysis chemistry and can supplement one another in heterogeneous catalysis. For instance, zeolites have the highest stability and hence can be readily used in gas phase catalysis, especially in industry catalysis, mesoporous silica enjoy the largest cavities and therefore allow large substrates to enter the pores, while MOF materials possess the biggest surface areas, i.e. a densest amount of active sites.97–99
To take advantage of catalytic behavior that is unique to MOFs, more attention might be paid to explore the application of MOF catalysts in more valuable organic molecule formation reactions, which can take place under relatively mild conditions. It is delightful to see more and more organic chemists have gotten involved in the field of MOF catalysis to open up new orientations of MOF catalysis. Some reactions, which cannot occur in the presence of free homogeneous catalysts, can work well after these homogeneous catalysts are incorporated/assembled within the MOF skeleton or pores due to the difference in electron configuration and the steric environment of the constituent ligands (or metalloligands) in confined spaces and the single molecule scattering in solution. Besides, they bring up challenges towards MOF catalysis. In the ligand-assisted transition metal catalyzed reactions, subtitle modification of the ligand might give rise to a huge improvement in the activity and selectivities including chemo-, regio- and enantio-selectivity. The same situation is suitable for catalytic reactions in the presence of organocatalysts. However, there are some practical difficulties to assemble/incorporate these high-valued ligands/organocatalysts within MOFs. This might account for the fact that the concepts of MOF catalysis have been set up for around twenty years; nevertheless, MOF materials may still have a long way to be considered as reliable heterogeneous catalysts in practical and industrial applications in general.
